{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "skarp",
          "version": "{toolVersion}",
          "semanticVersion": "{toolVersion}",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/sirphilliptubell/skarp-sharp",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "complex-method",
              "name": "ComplexMethod",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Much branching logic in one member makes it hard to follow and to test."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the control-flow complexity of one member. By default cognitive complexity, which adds one for each break in the linear flow (if, loops, switch, catch, jumps, runs of \u0026\u0026 and ||) and more for each level of nesting it sits in, whatever the preset: a preset without a cognitive threshold uses the default\u0027s cognitive threshold, credited to its source. Only \u0060--complex-metric cyclomatic\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline (or \u0022complexMetric\u0022: \u0022cyclomatic\u0022 in .skarp.json) reads cyclomatic complexity: the number of independent paths (1 plus each decision point). Lambdas and local functions are folded into the member they sit in."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "complex-method: Complex method (judged per member)\n\nMuch branching logic in one member makes it hard to follow and to test.\n\nWhat it measures: the control-flow complexity of one member. By default cognitive complexity, which adds one\nfor each break in the linear flow (if, loops, switch, catch, jumps, runs of \u0026\u0026 and ||) and more for each level\nof nesting it sits in, whatever the preset: a preset without a cognitive threshold uses the default\u0027s cognitive\nthreshold, credited to its source. Only \u0060--complex-metric cyclomatic\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline (or\n\u0022complexMetric\u0022: \u0022cyclomatic\u0022 in .skarp.json) reads cyclomatic complexity: the number of independent paths (1\nplus each decision point). Lambdas and local functions are folded into the member they sit in.\n\nWhy it matters: every extra decision is a case a reader must keep in mind and a test must cover. Members with\nhigh complexity are where misunderstandings and missed cases tend to collect.\n\nHow to improve: extract well-named methods for separate steps or branches, replace flag arguments and long\nif/else chains with early returns, guard clauses, a lookup table or polymorphism, and move deeply nested blocks\ninto their own members.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default [cognitive]\n    notice:  cognitive over 15  (Sonar S3776, PMD, detekt and Lizard: 15)\n    warning: blank\n  default [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 15  (ReportGenerator and NDepend: 15)\n    warning: cyclomatic over 30  (NDepend: 30, extremely complex)\n  codescene [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic at least 9  (CodeScene defaults: 9)\n    warning: cyclomatic at least 100  (CodeScene defaults: 100)\n  clean-cadet [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 10  (Clean-CaDET: 10, Liu 2012 rule)\n    warning: blank\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 15  (ReportGenerator: 15)\n    warning: blank\n  ndepend [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 15  (NDepend: 15, hard to maintain)\n    warning: cyclomatic over 30  (NDepend: 30, extremely complex)\n  pmd [cognitive]\n    notice:  cognitive at least 15  (PMD CognitiveComplexity: 15)\n    warning: blank\n  pmd [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic at least 10  (PMD CyclomaticComplexity: 10)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 10  (Checkstyle CyclomaticComplexity: 10)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomaticMicrosoft over 25  (Microsoft CA1502: 25, Microsoft counting)\n    warning: blank\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 10  (Yamashita et al.: 9-10, 80th percentile)\n    warning: cyclomatic over 19  (Yamashita et al.: 16-19, 90th percentile)\n\nChange a value: --threshold complex-method.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold complex-method.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.complex-method in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**complex-method: Complex method (judged per member)**\n\nMuch branching logic in one member makes it hard to follow and to test.\n\nWhat it measures: the control-flow complexity of one member. By default cognitive complexity, which adds one for each break in the linear flow (if, loops, switch, catch, jumps, runs of \u0026\u0026 and ||) and more for each level of nesting it sits in, whatever the preset: a preset without a cognitive threshold uses the default\u0027s cognitive threshold, credited to its source. Only \u0060--complex-metric cyclomatic\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline (or \u0022complexMetric\u0022: \u0022cyclomatic\u0022 in .skarp.json) reads cyclomatic complexity: the number of independent paths (1 plus each decision point). Lambdas and local functions are folded into the member they sit in.\n\nWhy it matters: every extra decision is a case a reader must keep in mind and a test must cover. Members with high complexity are where misunderstandings and missed cases tend to collect.\n\nHow to improve: extract well-named methods for separate steps or branches, replace flag arguments and long if/else chains with early returns, guard clauses, a lookup table or polymorphism, and move deeply nested blocks into their own members.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default [cognitive]\n    notice:  cognitive over 15  (Sonar S3776, PMD, detekt and Lizard: 15)\n    warning: blank\n  default [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 15  (ReportGenerator and NDepend: 15)\n    warning: cyclomatic over 30  (NDepend: 30, extremely complex)\n  codescene [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic at least 9  (CodeScene defaults: 9)\n    warning: cyclomatic at least 100  (CodeScene defaults: 100)\n  clean-cadet [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 10  (Clean-CaDET: 10, Liu 2012 rule)\n    warning: blank\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 15  (ReportGenerator: 15)\n    warning: blank\n  ndepend [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 15  (NDepend: 15, hard to maintain)\n    warning: cyclomatic over 30  (NDepend: 30, extremely complex)\n  pmd [cognitive]\n    notice:  cognitive at least 15  (PMD CognitiveComplexity: 15)\n    warning: blank\n  pmd [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic at least 10  (PMD CyclomaticComplexity: 10)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 10  (Checkstyle CyclomaticComplexity: 10)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomaticMicrosoft over 25  (Microsoft CA1502: 25, Microsoft counting)\n    warning: blank\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile [cyclomatic]\n    notice:  cyclomatic over 10  (Yamashita et al.: 9-10, 80th percentile)\n    warning: cyclomatic over 19  (Yamashita et al.: 16-19, 90th percentile)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold complex-method.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold complex-method.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.complex-method in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "deep-nesting",
              "name": "DeepNesting",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Code nested many levels deep forces the reader to keep every enclosing condition in mind."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the deepest nesting of control structures in the member (if, switch, loops, try), the outermost one being level 1. An else-if chain does not deepen; a lambda or local function adds one level for its body."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "deep-nesting: Deep nesting (judged per member)\n\nCode nested many levels deep forces the reader to keep every enclosing condition in mind.\n\nWhat it measures: the deepest nesting of control structures in the member (if, switch, loops, try), the outermost\none being level 1. An else-if chain does not deepen; a lambda or local function adds one level for its body.\n\nWhy it matters: each level is a condition the reader has to remember to understand the innermost line. Deeply\nnested code is also hard to change without breaking one of the enclosing cases.\n\nHow to improve: return early (guard clauses) instead of wrapping the rest of the body in an if, invert conditions,\nuse continue in loops, and extract the inner block into a method with a name that says what it does.\n\nNote: tools count levels differently (some count only if statements, some count IL blocks), so a borrowed number\ncomes with its own counting; skarp applies it to its own count (outermost construct = 1).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  nesting over 3  (CodeScene: depth 4)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene\n    notice:  nesting at least 4  (CodeScene defaults: 4)\n    warning: blank\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  nesting over 4  (NDepend: depth 4, counted on IL)\n    warning: nesting over 8  (NDepend: depth 8, counted on IL)\n  pmd\n    notice:  nesting at least 3  (PMD AvoidDeeplyNestedIfStmts: 3, if statements only)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  nesting over 2  (Checkstyle NestedIfDepth: 1, outermost 0, if statements only)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold deep-nesting.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold deep-nesting.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.deep-nesting in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**deep-nesting: Deep nesting (judged per member)**\n\nCode nested many levels deep forces the reader to keep every enclosing condition in mind.\n\nWhat it measures: the deepest nesting of control structures in the member (if, switch, loops, try), the outermost one being level 1. An else-if chain does not deepen; a lambda or local function adds one level for its body.\n\nWhy it matters: each level is a condition the reader has to remember to understand the innermost line. Deeply nested code is also hard to change without breaking one of the enclosing cases.\n\nHow to improve: return early (guard clauses) instead of wrapping the rest of the body in an if, invert conditions, use continue in loops, and extract the inner block into a method with a name that says what it does.\n\nNote: tools count levels differently (some count only if statements, some count IL blocks), so a borrowed number comes with its own counting; skarp applies it to its own count (outermost construct = 1).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  nesting over 3  (CodeScene: depth 4)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene\n    notice:  nesting at least 4  (CodeScene defaults: 4)\n    warning: blank\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  nesting over 4  (NDepend: depth 4, counted on IL)\n    warning: nesting over 8  (NDepend: depth 8, counted on IL)\n  pmd\n    notice:  nesting at least 3  (PMD AvoidDeeplyNestedIfStmts: 3, if statements only)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  nesting over 2  (Checkstyle NestedIfDepth: 1, outermost 0, if statements only)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold deep-nesting.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold deep-nesting.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.deep-nesting in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "long-method",
              "name": "LongMethod",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A long member usually does several things; it is harder to read, name, test and reuse."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the size of one member, by default its physical lines (every line of the declaration span, signature, blank and comment lines included: the unit of the Clean-CaDET labels the default threshold was measured on). \u0060--long-method-lines source\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline (or \u0022longMethodLines\u0022: \u0022source\u0022 in .skarp.json) counts only lines with code instead; the choice applies to every line threshold of the rule. Some presets count statements. Lambdas and local functions count as lines of the member they sit in; their own records are not judged by this rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "long-method: Long method (judged per member)\n\nA long member usually does several things; it is harder to read, name, test and reuse.\n\nWhat it measures: the size of one member, by default its physical lines (every line of the declaration span,\nsignature, blank and comment lines included: the unit of the Clean-CaDET labels the default threshold was\nmeasured on). \u0060--long-method-lines source\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline (or \u0022longMethodLines\u0022: \u0022source\u0022 in\n.skarp.json) counts only lines with code instead; the choice applies to every line threshold of the rule. Some\npresets count statements. Lambdas and local functions count as lines of the member they sit in; their own\nrecords are not judged by this rule.\n\nWhy it matters: a long member tends to mix several responsibilities and levels of abstraction. It is harder to\ngive it a precise name, to test its parts separately and to change one part without touching the others.\n\nHow to improve: look for blocks that already carry a comment or a blank line around them; those are often\nmethods waiting to be extracted. Split setup, work and reporting; move a large literal table or object initializer\ninto data or its own member.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  (\u0022lines\u0022 = physical lines of the member: every line of the declaration; to count only lines with code use\n   \u0060--long-method-lines source\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022longMethodLines\u0022: \u0022source\u0022 in .skarp.json)\n  default\n    notice:  lines over 50  (Clean-CaDET: 50 lines, measured on 2,574 C# methods)\n    warning: lines over 70  (CodeScene: 70 lines)\n  codescene\n    notice:  lines over 70  (CodeScene defaults: 70)\n    warning: lines over 500  (CodeScene defaults: 500)\n  clean-cadet\n    notice:  lines over 50  (Clean-CaDET: 50 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  doomen\n    notice:  statements over 15  (Doomen AV1500: 15 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  statements over 20  (NDepend: 20 logical lines, as statements)\n    warning: statements over 40  (NDepend: 40 logical lines, as statements)\n  pmd\n    notice:  statements at least 60  (PMD NcssCount: 60 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  lines over 150  (Checkstyle MethodLength: 150 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile\n    notice:  lines over 36  (Chowdhury et al.: 36 lines, 80th percentile)\n    warning: lines over 63  (Chowdhury et al.: 63 lines, 90th percentile)\n\nChange a value: --threshold long-method.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold long-method.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.long-method in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**long-method: Long method (judged per member)**\n\nA long member usually does several things; it is harder to read, name, test and reuse.\n\nWhat it measures: the size of one member, by default its physical lines (every line of the declaration span, signature, blank and comment lines included: the unit of the Clean-CaDET labels the default threshold was measured on). \u0060--long-method-lines source\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline (or \u0022longMethodLines\u0022: \u0022source\u0022 in .skarp.json) counts only lines with code instead; the choice applies to every line threshold of the rule. Some presets count statements. Lambdas and local functions count as lines of the member they sit in; their own records are not judged by this rule.\n\nWhy it matters: a long member tends to mix several responsibilities and levels of abstraction. It is harder to give it a precise name, to test its parts separately and to change one part without touching the others.\n\nHow to improve: look for blocks that already carry a comment or a blank line around them; those are often methods waiting to be extracted. Split setup, work and reporting; move a large literal table or object initializer into data or its own member.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  (\u0022lines\u0022 = physical lines of the member: every line of the declaration; to count only lines with code use\n   \u0060--long-method-lines source\u0060 on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022longMethodLines\u0022: \u0022source\u0022 in .skarp.json)\n  default\n    notice:  lines over 50  (Clean-CaDET: 50 lines, measured on 2,574 C# methods)\n    warning: lines over 70  (CodeScene: 70 lines)\n  codescene\n    notice:  lines over 70  (CodeScene defaults: 70)\n    warning: lines over 500  (CodeScene defaults: 500)\n  clean-cadet\n    notice:  lines over 50  (Clean-CaDET: 50 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  doomen\n    notice:  statements over 15  (Doomen AV1500: 15 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  statements over 20  (NDepend: 20 logical lines, as statements)\n    warning: statements over 40  (NDepend: 40 logical lines, as statements)\n  pmd\n    notice:  statements at least 60  (PMD NcssCount: 60 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  lines over 150  (Checkstyle MethodLength: 150 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile\n    notice:  lines over 36  (Chowdhury et al.: 36 lines, 80th percentile)\n    warning: lines over 63  (Chowdhury et al.: 63 lines, 90th percentile)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold long-method.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold long-method.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.long-method in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "large-type",
              "name": "LargeType",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A large class or struct often holds several responsibilities that could live apart."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the size of a class, struct, record or interface, all partial declarations merged: source lines of the declarations (nested types included), WMC (the sum of the members\u0027 cyclomatic complexity), methods (methods, operators and constructors) and fields, depending on the preset. Enums and delegates are not judged. The finding points at the first declaration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "large-type: Large type (judged per type)\n\nA large class or struct often holds several responsibilities that could live apart.\n\nWhat it measures: the size of a class, struct, record or interface, all partial declarations merged: source lines\nof the declarations (nested types included), WMC (the sum of the members\u0027 cyclomatic complexity), methods\n(methods, operators and constructors) and fields, depending on the preset. Enums and delegates are not judged.\nThe finding points at the first declaration.\n\nWhy it matters: big types attract more change and more people, and they make it hard to see which members belong\ntogether. They often hide a second or third concept.\n\nHow to improve: group members by the fields they use; a group that shares fields among itself but not with the\nrest is a candidate for its own type. Move helper logic to the types whose data it works on.\n\nNote: the default threshold (Clean-CaDET, 100 lines or WMC 20) was measured against human labels and is\ndeliberately sensitive, hence a notice only.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  sourceLines over 100 or wmc over 20  (Clean-CaDET: 100 lines or WMC 20, measured on 920 C# classes)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet\n    notice:  sourceLines over 100 or wmc over 20  (Clean-CaDET: 100 lines or WMC 20, Liu 2012 rule)\n    warning: blank\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  methods over 20 or fields over 15  (NDepend: 20 methods or 15 fields)\n    warning: blank\n  pmd\n    notice:  methods over 10 or fields over 15 or statements at least 1500  (PMD: 10 methods, 15 fields or 1500 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  methods over 100 or statements over 1500  (Checkstyle MethodCount: 100; JavaNCSS: 1500 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile\n    notice:  methods over 28  (Yamashita et al.: 25-28 methods, 80th percentile)\n    warning: methods over 47  (Yamashita et al.: 42-47 methods, 90th percentile)\n\nChange a value: --threshold large-type.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold large-type.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.large-type in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**large-type: Large type (judged per type)**\n\nA large class or struct often holds several responsibilities that could live apart.\n\nWhat it measures: the size of a class, struct, record or interface, all partial declarations merged: source lines of the declarations (nested types included), WMC (the sum of the members\u0027 cyclomatic complexity), methods (methods, operators and constructors) and fields, depending on the preset. Enums and delegates are not judged. The finding points at the first declaration.\n\nWhy it matters: big types attract more change and more people, and they make it hard to see which members belong together. They often hide a second or third concept.\n\nHow to improve: group members by the fields they use; a group that shares fields among itself but not with the rest is a candidate for its own type. Move helper logic to the types whose data it works on.\n\nNote: the default threshold (Clean-CaDET, 100 lines or WMC 20) was measured against human labels and is deliberately sensitive, hence a notice only.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  sourceLines over 100 or wmc over 20  (Clean-CaDET: 100 lines or WMC 20, measured on 920 C# classes)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet\n    notice:  sourceLines over 100 or wmc over 20  (Clean-CaDET: 100 lines or WMC 20, Liu 2012 rule)\n    warning: blank\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  methods over 20 or fields over 15  (NDepend: 20 methods or 15 fields)\n    warning: blank\n  pmd\n    notice:  methods over 10 or fields over 15 or statements at least 1500  (PMD: 10 methods, 15 fields or 1500 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  methods over 100 or statements over 1500  (Checkstyle MethodCount: 100; JavaNCSS: 1500 statements)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile\n    notice:  methods over 28  (Yamashita et al.: 25-28 methods, 80th percentile)\n    warning: methods over 47  (Yamashita et al.: 42-47 methods, 90th percentile)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold large-type.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold large-type.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.large-type in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "type"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "large-file",
              "name": "LargeFile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A very large source file is hard to navigate, review and merge."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: source lines of the whole file (some presets: physical lines). Each type in the file is judged separately by large-type."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "large-file: Large file (judged per file)\n\nA very large source file is hard to navigate, review and merge.\n\nWhat it measures: source lines of the whole file (some presets: physical lines). Each type in the file is judged\nseparately by large-type.\n\nWhy it matters: large files collect unrelated changes, which makes reviews and merges harder and hides structure.\n\nHow to improve: one top-level type per file, and split a large type (see large-type) or move nested helper types\ninto their own files.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  sourceLines over 1000  (CodeScene: 1000 lines)\n    warning: sourceLines over 5000  (CodeScene: 5000 lines, alert)\n  codescene\n    notice:  sourceLines over 1000  (CodeScene defaults: 1000)\n    warning: sourceLines over 5000  (CodeScene defaults: 5000)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  physicalLines over 2000  (Checkstyle FileLength: 2000 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile\n    notice:  sourceLines over 553  (Yamashita et al.: 466-553 lines, 80th percentile)\n    warning: sourceLines over 975  (Yamashita et al.: 847-975 lines, 90th percentile)\n\nChange a value: --threshold large-file.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold large-file.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.large-file in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**large-file: Large file (judged per file)**\n\nA very large source file is hard to navigate, review and merge.\n\nWhat it measures: source lines of the whole file (some presets: physical lines). Each type in the file is judged separately by large-type.\n\nWhy it matters: large files collect unrelated changes, which makes reviews and merges harder and hides structure.\n\nHow to improve: one top-level type per file, and split a large type (see large-type) or move nested helper types into their own files.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  sourceLines over 1000  (CodeScene: 1000 lines)\n    warning: sourceLines over 5000  (CodeScene: 5000 lines, alert)\n  codescene\n    notice:  sourceLines over 1000  (CodeScene defaults: 1000)\n    warning: sourceLines over 5000  (CodeScene defaults: 5000)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  physicalLines over 2000  (Checkstyle FileLength: 2000 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile\n    notice:  sourceLines over 553  (Yamashita et al.: 466-553 lines, 80th percentile)\n    warning: sourceLines over 975  (Yamashita et al.: 847-975 lines, 90th percentile)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold large-file.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold large-file.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.large-file in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "unit": "file"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "too-many-parameters",
              "name": "TooManyParameters",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Many parameters make calls hard to read and suggest the member does too much or misses a type."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: declared parameters of a method, constructor, operator, local function or indexer, and of a primary constructor (record R(int A, ...), class C(...)). The receiver of an extension method (this) is not counted; params and optional parameters count as one each. Overrides and explicit interface implementations are skipped (their signature is decided elsewhere); lambdas are skipped. Some presets allow constructors one more parameter (dependency injection)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "too-many-parameters: Too many parameters (judged per member)\n\nMany parameters make calls hard to read and suggest the member does too much or misses a type.\n\nWhat it measures: declared parameters of a method, constructor, operator, local function or indexer, and of a\nprimary constructor (record R(int A, ...), class C(...)). The receiver of an extension method (this) is not counted;\nparams and optional parameters count as one each. Overrides and explicit interface implementations are skipped\n(their signature is decided elsewhere); lambdas are skipped. Some presets allow constructors one more parameter\n(dependency injection).\n\nWhy it matters: long parameter lists are easy to call with arguments in the wrong order, and they often travel\ntogether through several layers, a sign of a missing concept.\n\nHow to improve: introduce a parameter object or record for values that belong together, split the member by\nresponsibility, or pass a collaborator instead of its parts. Wide positional records are often fine as data\ncarriers; consider whether the record mixes concerns.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  parameters over 4 or constructorParameters over 5  (CodeScene: 4 arguments, 5 for constructors)\n    warning: parameters at least 8  (NDepend: 8 or more)\n  codescene\n    notice:  parameters over 4 or constructorParameters over 5  (CodeScene defaults: 4, constructors 5)\n    warning: blank\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen\n    notice:  parameters over 3  (Doomen AV1561: 3 parameters)\n    warning: blank\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  parameters over 5  (NDepend: over 5 is painful)\n    warning: parameters at least 8  (NDepend: 8 or more)\n  pmd\n    notice:  parameters at least 10  (PMD ExcessiveParameterList: 10)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  parameters over 7  (Checkstyle ParameterNumber: 7)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold too-many-parameters.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold too-many-parameters.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.too-many-parameters in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**too-many-parameters: Too many parameters (judged per member)**\n\nMany parameters make calls hard to read and suggest the member does too much or misses a type.\n\nWhat it measures: declared parameters of a method, constructor, operator, local function or indexer, and of a primary constructor (record R(int A, ...), class C(...)). The receiver of an extension method (this) is not counted; params and optional parameters count as one each. Overrides and explicit interface implementations are skipped (their signature is decided elsewhere); lambdas are skipped. Some presets allow constructors one more parameter (dependency injection).\n\nWhy it matters: long parameter lists are easy to call with arguments in the wrong order, and they often travel together through several layers, a sign of a missing concept.\n\nHow to improve: introduce a parameter object or record for values that belong together, split the member by responsibility, or pass a collaborator instead of its parts. Wide positional records are often fine as data carriers; consider whether the record mixes concerns.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  parameters over 4 or constructorParameters over 5  (CodeScene: 4 arguments, 5 for constructors)\n    warning: parameters at least 8  (NDepend: 8 or more)\n  codescene\n    notice:  parameters over 4 or constructorParameters over 5  (CodeScene defaults: 4, constructors 5)\n    warning: blank\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen\n    notice:  parameters over 3  (Doomen AV1561: 3 parameters)\n    warning: blank\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  parameters over 5  (NDepend: over 5 is painful)\n    warning: parameters at least 8  (NDepend: 8 or more)\n  pmd\n    notice:  parameters at least 10  (PMD ExcessiveParameterList: 10)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  parameters over 7  (Checkstyle ParameterNumber: 7)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold too-many-parameters.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold too-many-parameters.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.too-many-parameters in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "path-explosion",
              "name": "PathExplosion",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The number of distinct paths through this member is so large that it cannot be tested path by path."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: NPath, the number of acyclic execution paths. Sequential decisions multiply (two independent if statements give 4 paths, ten give 1,024), nested ones add. Lambdas and local functions are their own records."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "path-explosion: Path explosion (NPath) (judged per member)\n\nThe number of distinct paths through this member is so large that it cannot be tested path by path.\n\nWhat it measures: NPath, the number of acyclic execution paths. Sequential decisions multiply (two independent\nif statements give 4 paths, ten give 1,024), nested ones add. Lambdas and local functions are their own records.\n\nWhy it matters: unlike cyclomatic complexity, NPath grows exponentially with independent decisions in sequence, so\nit shows members whose combinations of conditions cannot all be reasoned about or tested.\n\nHow to improve: extract independent decision blocks into their own methods (each then has a small NPath), replace\nlong chains of independent if statements with data-driven code, and avoid boolean flags that switch parts of the body.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  npath over 200  (Nejmeh 1988, ReportGenerator, Checkstyle and PMD: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator\n    notice:  npath over 200  (ReportGenerator: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd\n    notice:  npath at least 200  (PMD NPathComplexity: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  npath over 200  (Checkstyle NPathComplexity: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold path-explosion.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold path-explosion.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.path-explosion in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**path-explosion: Path explosion (NPath) (judged per member)**\n\nThe number of distinct paths through this member is so large that it cannot be tested path by path.\n\nWhat it measures: NPath, the number of acyclic execution paths. Sequential decisions multiply (two independent if statements give 4 paths, ten give 1,024), nested ones add. Lambdas and local functions are their own records.\n\nWhy it matters: unlike cyclomatic complexity, NPath grows exponentially with independent decisions in sequence, so it shows members whose combinations of conditions cannot all be reasoned about or tested.\n\nHow to improve: extract independent decision blocks into their own methods (each then has a small NPath), replace long chains of independent if statements with data-driven code, and avoid boolean flags that switch parts of the body.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  npath over 200  (Nejmeh 1988, ReportGenerator, Checkstyle and PMD: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator\n    notice:  npath over 200  (ReportGenerator: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd\n    notice:  npath at least 200  (PMD NPathComplexity: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  npath over 200  (Checkstyle NPathComplexity: 200)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold path-explosion.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold path-explosion.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.path-explosion in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "active-complex-file",
              "name": "ActiveComplexFile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "This file changed often recently and contains code other rules flag, so effort spent here pays back first."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: files in the top share (default 4 percent) of changed files by commits in the history window (12 months by default), which also have at least one complexity-type finding (rules complex-method, deep-nesting, long-method, large-type, large-file, too-many-parameters, path-explosion, bumpy-road, complex-condition). It is the activity hotspot table (skarp hotspots) as a finding. Needs git history; skipped with a note without it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "active-complex-file: Active complex file (judged per file)\n\nThis file changed often recently and contains code other rules flag, so effort spent here pays back first.\n\nWhat it measures: files in the top share (default 4 percent) of changed files by commits in the history window\n(12 months by default), which also have at least one complexity-type finding (rules complex-method, deep-nesting,\nlong-method, large-type, large-file, too-many-parameters, path-explosion, bumpy-road, complex-condition). It is the\nactivity hotspot table (skarp hotspots) as a finding. Needs git history; skipped with a note without it.\n\nWhy it matters: code that is both complex and frequently changed costs effort on every change. It does not say\nthe file is risky or buggy: it says it changed often and contains code the other rules flag.\n\nHow to improve: start improvements here, in small steps along with the planned changes; look at the file\u0027s other\nfindings first.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  activityShare at least 4  (CodeScene: 2 to 4 percent, Tornhill: 1 to 3 percent; Skarp uses 4)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: share=4\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold active-complex-file.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold active-complex-file.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold active-complex-file.share=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.active-complex-file in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**active-complex-file: Active complex file (judged per file)**\n\nThis file changed often recently and contains code other rules flag, so effort spent here pays back first.\n\nWhat it measures: files in the top share (default 4 percent) of changed files by commits in the history window (12 months by default), which also have at least one complexity-type finding (rules complex-method, deep-nesting, long-method, large-type, large-file, too-many-parameters, path-explosion, bumpy-road, complex-condition). It is the activity hotspot table (skarp hotspots) as a finding. Needs git history; skipped with a note without it.\n\nWhy it matters: code that is both complex and frequently changed costs effort on every change. It does not say the file is risky or buggy: it says it changed often and contains code the other rules flag.\n\nHow to improve: start improvements here, in small steps along with the planned changes; look at the file\u0027s other findings first.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  activityShare at least 4  (CodeScene: 2 to 4 percent, Tornhill: 1 to 3 percent; Skarp uses 4)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: share=4\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold active-complex-file.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold active-complex-file.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold active-complex-file.share=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.active-complex-file in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "file"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bumpy-road",
              "name": "BumpyRoad",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Several separate blocks of nested logic in one member: each bump is a missing abstraction."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the top-level statements of the member body (try, using, lock and plain blocks are looked through) are scanned for if, switch and loop statements whose nested conditional or loop logic reaches the bump depth (default 2, the statement itself being level 1). Adjacent such statements form one chunk; the number of separate chunks is the number of bumps. The idea is CodeScene\u0027s Bumpy Road smell; the exact detection is ours."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "bumpy-road: Bumpy road (judged per member)\n\nSeveral separate blocks of nested logic in one member: each bump is a missing abstraction.\n\nWhat it measures: the top-level statements of the member body (try, using, lock and plain blocks are looked\nthrough) are scanned for if, switch and loop statements whose nested conditional or loop logic reaches the bump\ndepth (default 2, the statement itself being level 1). Adjacent such statements form one chunk; the number of\nseparate chunks is the number of bumps. The idea is CodeScene\u0027s Bumpy Road smell; the exact detection is ours.\n\nWhy it matters: every bump asks the reader to build a mental model of another nested block, and the blocks often\nshare state through local variables, which makes them hard to change one at a time.\n\nHow to improve: extract each bump into a well-named method (Extract Method); often the bumps are steps of an\nalgorithm that read better as a sequence of calls.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  bumps at least 2  (CodeScene: 2 bumps at depth 2)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: depth=2\n  codescene\n    notice:  bumps at least 2  (CodeScene defaults: 2 bumps at depth 2)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: depth=2\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold bumpy-road.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold bumpy-road.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold bumpy-road.depth=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.bumpy-road in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**bumpy-road: Bumpy road (judged per member)**\n\nSeveral separate blocks of nested logic in one member: each bump is a missing abstraction.\n\nWhat it measures: the top-level statements of the member body (try, using, lock and plain blocks are looked through) are scanned for if, switch and loop statements whose nested conditional or loop logic reaches the bump depth (default 2, the statement itself being level 1). Adjacent such statements form one chunk; the number of separate chunks is the number of bumps. The idea is CodeScene\u0027s Bumpy Road smell; the exact detection is ours.\n\nWhy it matters: every bump asks the reader to build a mental model of another nested block, and the blocks often share state through local variables, which makes them hard to change one at a time.\n\nHow to improve: extract each bump into a well-named method (Extract Method); often the bumps are steps of an algorithm that read better as a sequence of calls.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  bumps at least 2  (CodeScene: 2 bumps at depth 2)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: depth=2\n  codescene\n    notice:  bumps at least 2  (CodeScene defaults: 2 bumps at depth 2)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: depth=2\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold bumpy-road.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold bumpy-road.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold bumpy-road.depth=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.bumpy-road in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "complex-condition",
              "name": "ComplexCondition",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A boolean expression with many \u0026\u0026 and || operators is hard to read and easy to get wrong."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the number of boolean operators in one branch condition: the condition of if, while, do, for and ?:, and the when clause of a switch case, a switch-expression arm or a catch clause. Other boolean expressions (assignments, returns, arguments, lambda bodies) are not counted, so a condition extracted into a named variable is not flagged. Counted: \u0026\u0026 and ||, and the pattern combinators and and or inside an is-pattern (not is not counted), across parentheses and negations; each operator counts, also in a run of the same operator. Patterns of case labels and switch arms are value lists, not conditions. The member reports its worst condition."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "complex-condition: Complex condition (judged per member)\n\nA boolean expression with many \u0026\u0026 and || operators is hard to read and easy to get wrong.\n\nWhat it measures: the number of boolean operators in one branch condition: the condition of if, while, do, for\nand ?:, and the when clause of a switch case, a switch-expression arm or a catch clause. Other boolean\nexpressions (assignments, returns, arguments, lambda bodies) are not counted, so a condition extracted into a\nnamed variable is not flagged. Counted: \u0026\u0026 and ||, and the pattern combinators and and or inside an is-pattern\n(not is not counted), across parentheses and negations; each operator counts, also in a run of the same operator.\nPatterns of case labels and switch arms are value lists, not conditions. The member reports its worst condition.\n\nWhy it matters: each operator doubles the combinations a reader must check, and mixed \u0026\u0026 and || without clear\ngrouping is a frequent source of logic errors.\n\nHow to improve: give the parts names (local variables or small methods such as IsEligible(order)), split one\ncondition into early returns, or move the decision to the type that owns the data.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  booleanOperators at least 2  (CodeScene: 2; Designite: 3 boolean sub-expressions)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene\n    notice:  booleanOperators at least 2  (CodeScene defaults: 2)\n    warning: blank\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  booleanOperators over 3  (Checkstyle BooleanExpressionComplexity: 3)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold complex-condition.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold complex-condition.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.complex-condition in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**complex-condition: Complex condition (judged per member)**\n\nA boolean expression with many \u0026\u0026 and || operators is hard to read and easy to get wrong.\n\nWhat it measures: the number of boolean operators in one branch condition: the condition of if, while, do, for and ?:, and the when clause of a switch case, a switch-expression arm or a catch clause. Other boolean expressions (assignments, returns, arguments, lambda bodies) are not counted, so a condition extracted into a named variable is not flagged. Counted: \u0026\u0026 and ||, and the pattern combinators and and or inside an is-pattern (not is not counted), across parentheses and negations; each operator counts, also in a run of the same operator. Patterns of case labels and switch arms are value lists, not conditions. The member reports its worst condition.\n\nWhy it matters: each operator doubles the combinations a reader must check, and mixed \u0026\u0026 and || without clear grouping is a frequent source of logic errors.\n\nHow to improve: give the parts names (local variables or small methods such as IsEligible(order)), split one condition into early returns, or move the decision to the type that owns the data.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  booleanOperators at least 2  (CodeScene: 2; Designite: 3 boolean sub-expressions)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene\n    notice:  booleanOperators at least 2  (CodeScene defaults: 2)\n    warning: blank\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  booleanOperators over 3  (Checkstyle BooleanExpressionComplexity: 3)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold complex-condition.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold complex-condition.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.complex-condition in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "empty-catch",
              "name": "EmptyCatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An empty catch silently swallows errors, so failures go unnoticed."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: catch clauses whose block has no statement. Severity (Skarp\u0027s own design; no source defines levels): warning for an empty block without any comment that catches everything (bare catch, catch (Exception) or catch (System.Exception)) without a when filter; notice when a comment documents the intent, when a specific exception type is caught, or when a filter narrows it. catch (OperationCanceledException) { } is ordinary in cancellation code and stays a notice."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "empty-catch: Empty catch block (judged per member)\n\nAn empty catch silently swallows errors, so failures go unnoticed.\n\nWhat it measures: catch clauses whose block has no statement. Severity (Skarp\u0027s own design; no source defines levels):\nwarning for an empty block without any comment that catches everything (bare catch, catch (Exception) or\ncatch (System.Exception)) without a when filter; notice when a comment documents the intent, when a specific\nexception type is caught, or when a filter narrows it. catch (OperationCanceledException) { } is ordinary in\ncancellation code and stays a notice.\n\nWhy it matters: swallowed exceptions hide real failures and make problems surface far from their cause.\n\nHow to improve: catch the specific exception you expect, log or handle it, or let it propagate. When ignoring is\nreally right, say why in a comment so the next reader does not have to guess.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own, after Checkstyle EmptyCatchBlock and Doomen AV1210)\n    warning: structural  (Skarp\u0027s own, after Checkstyle EmptyCatchBlock and Doomen AV1210)\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold empty-catch.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold empty-catch.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.empty-catch in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**empty-catch: Empty catch block (judged per member)**\n\nAn empty catch silently swallows errors, so failures go unnoticed.\n\nWhat it measures: catch clauses whose block has no statement. Severity (Skarp\u0027s own design; no source defines levels): warning for an empty block without any comment that catches everything (bare catch, catch (Exception) or catch (System.Exception)) without a when filter; notice when a comment documents the intent, when a specific exception type is caught, or when a filter narrows it. catch (OperationCanceledException) { } is ordinary in cancellation code and stays a notice.\n\nWhy it matters: swallowed exceptions hide real failures and make problems surface far from their cause.\n\nHow to improve: catch the specific exception you expect, log or handle it, or let it propagate. When ignoring is really right, say why in a comment so the next reader does not have to guess.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own, after Checkstyle EmptyCatchBlock and Doomen AV1210)\n    warning: structural  (Skarp\u0027s own, after Checkstyle EmptyCatchBlock and Doomen AV1210)\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold empty-catch.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold empty-catch.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.empty-catch in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "magic-number",
              "name": "MagicNumber",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unexplained numeric literals hide their meaning; a named constant says why the value is what it is."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: numeric literals in member bodies, one finding per member with the count. Not counted: values in the ignore list (default -1, 0, 1, 2), local const declarations, attribute arguments, default parameter values, element-access arguments such as [3], array sizes (new byte[4096], stackalloc char[256]), and multiplication or division by a power of ten (unit conversions such as * 1000). Field, property and enum initializers are not scanned (they name the value already). Notice level only."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "magic-number: Magic numbers (judged per member)\n\nUnexplained numeric literals hide their meaning; a named constant says why the value is what it is.\n\nWhat it measures: numeric literals in member bodies, one finding per member with the count. Not counted: values\nin the ignore list (default -1, 0, 1, 2), local const declarations, attribute arguments, default parameter values,\nelement-access arguments such as [3], array sizes (new byte[4096], stackalloc char[256]), and multiplication or\ndivision by a power of ten (unit conversions such as * 1000). Field, property and enum initializers are not scanned (they name the value already). Notice level only.\n\nWhy it matters: a bare 3600 or 0.075 tells the reader nothing about what it stands for, and the same value\ncopied in several places drifts apart when one copy changes.\n\nHow to improve: introduce a named constant, an enum member or a configuration value, and use it everywhere the\nvalue means the same thing.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Checkstyle MagicNumber: ignores -1, 0, 1, 2; positions after Doomen AV1515)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: ignore=-1,0,1,2\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  structural  (Checkstyle MagicNumber: ignores -1, 0, 1, 2)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: ignore=-1,0,1,2\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold magic-number.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold magic-number.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold magic-number.ignore=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.magic-number in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**magic-number: Magic numbers (judged per member)**\n\nUnexplained numeric literals hide their meaning; a named constant says why the value is what it is.\n\nWhat it measures: numeric literals in member bodies, one finding per member with the count. Not counted: values in the ignore list (default -1, 0, 1, 2), local const declarations, attribute arguments, default parameter values, element-access arguments such as [3], array sizes (new byte[4096], stackalloc char[256]), and multiplication or division by a power of ten (unit conversions such as * 1000). Field, property and enum initializers are not scanned (they name the value already). Notice level only.\n\nWhy it matters: a bare 3600 or 0.075 tells the reader nothing about what it stands for, and the same value copied in several places drifts apart when one copy changes.\n\nHow to improve: introduce a named constant, an enum member or a configuration value, and use it everywhere the value means the same thing.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Checkstyle MagicNumber: ignores -1, 0, 1, 2; positions after Doomen AV1515)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: ignore=-1,0,1,2\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  structural  (Checkstyle MagicNumber: ignores -1, 0, 1, 2)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: ignore=-1,0,1,2\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold magic-number.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold magic-number.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold magic-number.ignore=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.magic-number in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "duplicate-code",
              "name": "DuplicateCode",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The same code, exact or with renamed identifiers and changed literals, appears in several places; a fix in one copy is easily missed in the others."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: copied code. The Roslyn tokens of every file (comments, whitespace and inactive #if text are not tokens; using directives, attribute lists and namespace declarations are left out) are compared with identifiers renamed consistently (Baker\u0027s parameterized matching: a = b \u002B c matches x = y \u002B z, but not x = x \u002B x) and literals reduced to their kind, so exact copies (kind exact) and copies with renamed names or changed values (kind renamed) are found. Copies are trimmed to whole statements and to whole members at their ends; a group whose copies lie mostly outside method, accessor, constructor and operator bodies (runs of property or field declarations) is dropped."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "duplicate-code: Duplicate code (judged per clone group)\n\nThe same code, exact or with renamed identifiers and changed literals, appears in several places; a fix in one copy is easily missed in the others.\n\nWhat it measures: copied code. The Roslyn tokens of every file (comments, whitespace and inactive #if text are\nnot tokens; using directives, attribute lists and namespace declarations are left out) are compared with\nidentifiers renamed consistently (Baker\u0027s parameterized matching: a = b \u002B c matches x = y \u002B z, but not x = x \u002B x)\nand literals reduced to their kind, so exact copies (kind exact) and copies with renamed names or changed values\n(kind renamed) are found. Copies are trimmed to whole statements and to whole members at their ends; a group\nwhose copies lie mostly outside method, accessor, constructor and operator bodies (runs of property or field\ndeclarations) is dropped. Low-content groups are skipped too: copies that average fewer than 5 tokens per line\nand where fewer than 40% of the lines differ in shape (brace-only lines not counted), such as constructors that\nonly store injected services (_a = a;), lookup or switch tables and migration builder chains; set\n--threshold duplicate-code.lowContent=report on skarp analyze or baseline (or thresholds.duplicate-code.lowContent\nin .skarp.json) to report them. One finding per clone group (two or more copies; a file may hold several), pointing at\nthe ordinal-first copy; the JSON clone block lists every copy. Default minimum: 100 tokens and 10 lines per copy\n(SonarQube\u0027s non-Java rule, the CPD documentation example); preset jscpd: 50 tokens and 5 lines. A warning when a\ncopy has 50 or more lines, or when 3 or more copies have copyLines (20) or more lines. The value is the lines of\nall copies together, so a group that gains a copy or grows is worsened against the baseline. Test files follow\nthe test setting; clones between test and production code are never reported; generated files are skipped. Clones\nare matched on the base view only (the parse with no symbols): code under #if branches it leaves inactive is not\nseen, even where other rules analyze it in a symbol variant. skarp check leaves the rule out (one\nfile cannot decide it); analyze, the Stop hook and the reports report it.\n\nWhy it matters: every copy must be found and changed when the logic changes; copies drift apart and a fix lands in\none of them only. Duplicated code is also extra code to read, review and test.\n\nHow to improve: extract the shared part into one method (with parameters for what differs), a base class, a\ngeneric method or a helper type, and call it from each place.\nDeliberate duplication: refactor real copies into one shared member. When the duplication is deliberate (generated\nshapes, per-type overloads an API repeats on purpose, test data), mark it on the member or type that holds a copy:\n    [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022duplicate-code\u0022, Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022)]\n(System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis; UnconditionalSuppressMessage works too). A group is left out when every copy is\nin suppressed code; with some copies suppressed, the others still form a group when two or more remain. A\nsuppression without a justification does not count. Suppressed findings are counted in the run summary and listed\nin the JSON (suppressed).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  tokens at least 100 and lines at least 10  (SonarQube: 100 tokens over at least 10 lines; PMD CPD: 100 tokens)\n    warning: lines at least 50 or copies at least 3  (Skarp\u0027s own: a copy of 50 or more lines, or 3 or more copies of copyLines (20) or more lines)\n    parameters: copyLines=20, lowContent=skip\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd\n    notice:  tokens at least 50 and lines at least 5  (jscpd: 50 tokens and 5 lines)\n    warning: lines at least 50 or copies at least 3  (Skarp\u0027s own: a copy of 50 or more lines, or 3 or more copies of copyLines (20) or more lines)\n    parameters: copyLines=20, lowContent=skip\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold duplicate-code.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold duplicate-code.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold duplicate-code.copyLines=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.duplicate-code in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**duplicate-code: Duplicate code (judged per clone group)**\n\nThe same code, exact or with renamed identifiers and changed literals, appears in several places; a fix in one copy is easily missed in the others.\n\nWhat it measures: copied code. The Roslyn tokens of every file (comments, whitespace and inactive #if text are not tokens; using directives, attribute lists and namespace declarations are left out) are compared with identifiers renamed consistently (Baker\u0027s parameterized matching: a = b \u002B c matches x = y \u002B z, but not x = x \u002B x) and literals reduced to their kind, so exact copies (kind exact) and copies with renamed names or changed values (kind renamed) are found. Copies are trimmed to whole statements and to whole members at their ends; a group whose copies lie mostly outside method, accessor, constructor and operator bodies (runs of property or field declarations) is dropped. Low-content groups are skipped too: copies that average fewer than 5 tokens per line and where fewer than 40% of the lines differ in shape (brace-only lines not counted), such as constructors that only store injected services (_a = a;), lookup or switch tables and migration builder chains; set --threshold duplicate-code.lowContent=report on skarp analyze or baseline (or thresholds.duplicate-code.lowContent in .skarp.json) to report them. One finding per clone group (two or more copies; a file may hold several), pointing at the ordinal-first copy; the JSON clone block lists every copy. Default minimum: 100 tokens and 10 lines per copy (SonarQube\u0027s non-Java rule, the CPD documentation example); preset jscpd: 50 tokens and 5 lines. A warning when a copy has 50 or more lines, or when 3 or more copies have copyLines (20) or more lines. The value is the lines of all copies together, so a group that gains a copy or grows is worsened against the baseline. Test files follow the test setting; clones between test and production code are never reported; generated files are skipped. Clones are matched on the base view only (the parse with no symbols): code under #if branches it leaves inactive is not seen, even where other rules analyze it in a symbol variant. skarp check leaves the rule out (one file cannot decide it); analyze, the Stop hook and the reports report it.\n\nWhy it matters: every copy must be found and changed when the logic changes; copies drift apart and a fix lands in one of them only. Duplicated code is also extra code to read, review and test.\n\nHow to improve: extract the shared part into one method (with parameters for what differs), a base class, a generic method or a helper type, and call it from each place. Deliberate duplication: refactor real copies into one shared member. When the duplication is deliberate (generated shapes, per-type overloads an API repeats on purpose, test data), mark it on the member or type that holds a copy: [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022duplicate-code\u0022, Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022)] (System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis; UnconditionalSuppressMessage works too). A group is left out when every copy is in suppressed code; with some copies suppressed, the others still form a group when two or more remain. A suppression without a justification does not count. Suppressed findings are counted in the run summary and listed in the JSON (suppressed).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  tokens at least 100 and lines at least 10  (SonarQube: 100 tokens over at least 10 lines; PMD CPD: 100 tokens)\n    warning: lines at least 50 or copies at least 3  (Skarp\u0027s own: a copy of 50 or more lines, or 3 or more copies of copyLines (20) or more lines)\n    parameters: copyLines=20, lowContent=skip\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd\n    notice:  tokens at least 50 and lines at least 5  (jscpd: 50 tokens and 5 lines)\n    warning: lines at least 50 or copies at least 3  (Skarp\u0027s own: a copy of 50 or more lines, or 3 or more copies of copyLines (20) or more lines)\n    parameters: copyLines=20, lowContent=skip\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold duplicate-code.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold duplicate-code.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off, --threshold duplicate-code.copyLines=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.duplicate-code in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "unit": "clone group"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "duplicate-code-near",
              "name": "DuplicateCodeNear",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Whole methods that are copies with small edits (statements added, removed or changed); notice only, score weight 0."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures (on by default, notice only, score weight 0; --rule duplicate-code-near=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022duplicate-code-near\u0022: false } in .skarp.json, switches it off): whole methods, constructors, operators and accessors that are copies with small edits (Type 3: statements added, removed or changed). Each method\u0027s syntax tree becomes a multiset of normalized subtree fingerprints (local names become one marker; called and accessed member names, type names and keywords stay); two methods are similar when the multiset Jaccard similarity is at least 0.75 and both have at least 10 lines (Skarp\u0027s own choice, which keeps boilerplate matches down). Methods that only forward a call are skipped; a pair that a duplicate-code group already covers is not reported again. Similar pairs are merged into groups; one finding per group, notice level only, with each pair\u0027s similarity in the JSON clone block (kind near-miss)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "duplicate-code-near: Near-duplicate methods (judged per clone group)\n\nWhole methods that are copies with small edits (statements added, removed or changed); notice only, score weight 0.\n\nWhat it measures (on by default, notice only, score weight 0; --rule duplicate-code-near=off on skarp analyze or\nbaseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022duplicate-code-near\u0022: false } in .skarp.json, switches it off): whole methods,\nconstructors, operators and accessors that are copies with small edits (Type 3: statements added, removed or\nchanged). Each method\u0027s syntax tree becomes a multiset of normalized subtree fingerprints (local names become one\nmarker; called and accessed member names, type names and keywords stay); two methods are similar when the\nmultiset Jaccard similarity is at least 0.75 and both have at least 10 lines (Skarp\u0027s own choice, which keeps boilerplate matches down). Methods that only forward a call are skipped; a pair that a duplicate-code group already\ncovers is not reported again. Similar pairs are merged into groups; one finding per group, notice level only,\nwith each pair\u0027s similarity in the JSON clone block (kind near-miss). On by default because another code health\ntool ships it on for C# (CodeScene\u0027s function-level Code Duplication: 10 lines, 75% similar); weight 0 in the\ncode health score because many of the groups are boilerplate of one shape, so it informs without lowering file scores, and as a notice\nit never enters the default gate.\n\nWhy it matters: near copies are the copies that already drifted apart; a fix in one often belongs in the others.\n\nHow to improve: compare the methods, extract the common steps, and pass in what differs (a parameter, a delegate,\na strategy).\nDeliberate duplication: refactor real copies into one shared member. When the duplication is deliberate (generated\nshapes, per-type overloads an API repeats on purpose, test data), mark it on the member or type that holds a copy:\n    [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022duplicate-code\u0022, Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022)]\n(System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis; UnconditionalSuppressMessage works too). A group is left out when every copy is\nin suppressed code; with some copies suppressed, the others still form a group when two or more remain. A\nsuppression without a justification does not count. Suppressed findings are counted in the run summary and listed\nin the JSON (suppressed).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  similarity at least 0.75 and lines at least 10  (Skarp\u0027s own, after CodeScene\u0027s method similarity: 0.75 similar and 10 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold duplicate-code-near.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold duplicate-code-near.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.duplicate-code-near in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**duplicate-code-near: Near-duplicate methods (judged per clone group)**\n\nWhole methods that are copies with small edits (statements added, removed or changed); notice only, score weight 0.\n\nWhat it measures (on by default, notice only, score weight 0; --rule duplicate-code-near=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022duplicate-code-near\u0022: false } in .skarp.json, switches it off): whole methods, constructors, operators and accessors that are copies with small edits (Type 3: statements added, removed or changed). Each method\u0027s syntax tree becomes a multiset of normalized subtree fingerprints (local names become one marker; called and accessed member names, type names and keywords stay); two methods are similar when the multiset Jaccard similarity is at least 0.75 and both have at least 10 lines (Skarp\u0027s own choice, which keeps boilerplate matches down). Methods that only forward a call are skipped; a pair that a duplicate-code group already covers is not reported again. Similar pairs are merged into groups; one finding per group, notice level only, with each pair\u0027s similarity in the JSON clone block (kind near-miss). On by default because another code health tool ships it on for C# (CodeScene\u0027s function-level Code Duplication: 10 lines, 75% similar); weight 0 in the code health score because many of the groups are boilerplate of one shape, so it informs without lowering file scores, and as a notice it never enters the default gate.\n\nWhy it matters: near copies are the copies that already drifted apart; a fix in one often belongs in the others.\n\nHow to improve: compare the methods, extract the common steps, and pass in what differs (a parameter, a delegate, a strategy). Deliberate duplication: refactor real copies into one shared member. When the duplication is deliberate (generated shapes, per-type overloads an API repeats on purpose, test data), mark it on the member or type that holds a copy: [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022duplicate-code\u0022, Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022)] (System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis; UnconditionalSuppressMessage works too). A group is left out when every copy is in suppressed code; with some copies suppressed, the others still form a group when two or more remain. A suppression without a justification does not count. Suppressed findings are counted in the run summary and listed in the JSON (suppressed).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  similarity at least 0.75 and lines at least 10  (Skarp\u0027s own, after CodeScene\u0027s method similarity: 0.75 similar and 10 lines)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold duplicate-code-near.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold duplicate-code-near.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.duplicate-code-near in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "clone group"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "suppression-without-justification",
              "name": "SuppressionWithoutJustification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, ...)] without a Justification does not count: say why the finding is acceptable."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: a [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022\u003Crule\u003E\u0022)] or UnconditionalSuppressMessage attribute (category skarp or skarp.\u003Canything\u003E) without a Justification, or with an empty one. Such a suppression does not count: the findings it would hide are reported. The finding points at the attribute. Notice level only; it does not lower the health score."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "suppression-without-justification: Suppression without justification (judged per member)\n\nA [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, ...)] without a Justification does not count: say why the finding is acceptable.\n\nWhat it measures: a [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022\u003Crule\u003E\u0022)] or UnconditionalSuppressMessage attribute (category\nskarp or skarp.\u003Canything\u003E) without a Justification, or with an empty one. Such a suppression does not count: the\nfindings it would hide are reported. The finding points at the attribute. Notice level only; it does not lower\nthe health score.\n\nWhy it matters: a suppression hides a finding from the reports, the gate and the score. The reason belongs next to\nit, so the next reader can judge whether it still holds.\n\nHow to improve: add Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022 to the attribute, or remove the attribute and deal with the finding.\n\nInline suppression works for every rule: on a member (its findings) or a type (the type\u0027s own findings and those\nof its members), or at assembly level with a Target (~T:Namespace.Type, ~M:Namespace.Type.Method, a namespace\nwith Scope \u0022namespace\u0022). skarp check sees only the edited file, so an assembly-level suppression in another file\n(GlobalSuppressions.cs) is not applied there. File-level findings (large-file, active-complex-file) cannot be\nsuppressed inline; use --exclude on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022exclude\u0022 in .skarp.json.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: a suppression must say why)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold suppression-without-justification.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold suppression-without-justification.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.suppression-without-justification in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**suppression-without-justification: Suppression without justification (judged per member)**\n\nA [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, ...)] without a Justification does not count: say why the finding is acceptable.\n\nWhat it measures: a [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022\u003Crule\u003E\u0022)] or UnconditionalSuppressMessage attribute (category skarp or skarp.\u003Canything\u003E) without a Justification, or with an empty one. Such a suppression does not count: the findings it would hide are reported. The finding points at the attribute. Notice level only; it does not lower the health score.\n\nWhy it matters: a suppression hides a finding from the reports, the gate and the score. The reason belongs next to it, so the next reader can judge whether it still holds.\n\nHow to improve: add Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022 to the attribute, or remove the attribute and deal with the finding.\n\nInline suppression works for every rule: on a member (its findings) or a type (the type\u0027s own findings and those of its members), or at assembly level with a Target (~T:Namespace.Type, ~M:Namespace.Type.Method, a namespace with Scope \u0022namespace\u0022). skarp check sees only the edited file, so an assembly-level suppression in another file (GlobalSuppressions.cs) is not applied there. File-level findings (large-file, active-complex-file) cannot be suppressed inline; use --exclude on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022exclude\u0022 in .skarp.json.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: a suppression must say why)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold suppression-without-justification.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold suppression-without-justification.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.suppression-without-justification in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "high-coupling",
              "name": "HighCoupling",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "This type uses many other types, so changes in any of them can ripple into it and it is hard to reuse alone."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: class coupling (CBO, efferent coupling) of a class, struct, record or interface, all partial declarations merged: the number of distinct other types it uses in its base list, signatures, fields, properties, attributes and code (local variables, object creation, casts, static and extension calls, generic arguments, patterns and catch types). Arrays, nullable values and tuples count their element types; keyword types (int, string, object, decimal, ...), the type itself, its containing and its own nested types do not. By default .NET framework types (namespace System and below) are not counted; cboAll counts them, and the microsoft preset uses Microsoft\u0027s own counting (Visual Studio code metrics, CA1506). Nested types are measured on their own; enums and delegates are not judged. Needs the semantic tier (a compilation): types touching unresolved symbols are not evaluated."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "high-coupling: High class coupling (judged per type)\n\nThis type uses many other types, so changes in any of them can ripple into it and it is hard to reuse alone.\n\nWhat it measures: class coupling (CBO, efferent coupling) of a class, struct, record or interface, all partial\ndeclarations merged: the number of distinct other types it uses in its base list, signatures, fields,\nproperties, attributes and code (local variables, object creation, casts, static and extension calls, generic\narguments, patterns and catch types). Arrays, nullable values and tuples count their element types; keyword\ntypes (int, string, object, decimal, ...), the type itself, its containing and its own nested types do not.\nBy default .NET framework types (namespace System and below) are not counted; cboAll counts them, and\nthe microsoft preset uses Microsoft\u0027s own counting (Visual Studio code metrics, CA1506). Nested types are\nmeasured on their own; enums and delegates are not judged. Needs the semantic tier (a compilation): types\ntouching unresolved symbols are not evaluated.\n\nWhy it matters: every type used is a reason to change and a dependency to understand, test and mock. Types\nthat use many others sit in the middle of many change paths and are hard to reuse or replace.\n\nHow to improve: move behaviour next to the data it uses, split the type by responsibility (groups of members\nthat use different collaborators), pass a smaller abstraction instead of several concrete types, or introduce\na facade for a group of collaborators that are always used together.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  cbo over 20  (Checkstyle ClassFanOutComplexity and PMD CouplingBetweenObjects: 20)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  cboAll over 50  (NDepend TypeCe: 50, third-party types counted)\n    warning: blank\n  pmd\n    notice:  cbo over 20  (PMD CouplingBetweenObjects: 20)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  cbo over 20  (Checkstyle ClassFanOutComplexity: 20)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft\n    notice:  cboMicrosoft over 95  (Microsoft CA1506: 95, Microsoft counting)\n    warning: blank\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nChange a value: --threshold high-coupling.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold high-coupling.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.high-coupling in .skarp.json.",
                "markdown": "**high-coupling: High class coupling (judged per type)**\n\nThis type uses many other types, so changes in any of them can ripple into it and it is hard to reuse alone.\n\nWhat it measures: class coupling (CBO, efferent coupling) of a class, struct, record or interface, all partial declarations merged: the number of distinct other types it uses in its base list, signatures, fields, properties, attributes and code (local variables, object creation, casts, static and extension calls, generic arguments, patterns and catch types). Arrays, nullable values and tuples count their element types; keyword types (int, string, object, decimal, ...), the type itself, its containing and its own nested types do not. By default .NET framework types (namespace System and below) are not counted; cboAll counts them, and the microsoft preset uses Microsoft\u0027s own counting (Visual Studio code metrics, CA1506). Nested types are measured on their own; enums and delegates are not judged. Needs the semantic tier (a compilation): types touching unresolved symbols are not evaluated.\n\nWhy it matters: every type used is a reason to change and a dependency to understand, test and mock. Types that use many others sit in the middle of many change paths and are hard to reuse or replace.\n\nHow to improve: move behaviour next to the data it uses, split the type by responsibility (groups of members that use different collaborators), pass a smaller abstraction instead of several concrete types, or introduce a facade for a group of collaborators that are always used together.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  cbo over 20  (Checkstyle ClassFanOutComplexity and PMD CouplingBetweenObjects: 20)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend\n    notice:  cboAll over 50  (NDepend TypeCe: 50, third-party types counted)\n    warning: blank\n  pmd\n    notice:  cbo over 20  (PMD CouplingBetweenObjects: 20)\n    warning: blank\n  checkstyle\n    notice:  cbo over 20  (Checkstyle ClassFanOutComplexity: 20)\n    warning: blank\n  microsoft\n    notice:  cboMicrosoft over 95  (Microsoft CA1506: 95, Microsoft counting)\n    warning: blank\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nChange a value: --threshold high-coupling.notice=\u003Cn\u003E, --threshold high-coupling.warning=\u003Cn\u003E or =off on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.high-coupling in .skarp.json."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "semantic"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "type"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "god-class",
              "name": "GodClass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A complex class that uses other classes\u0027 data and has low cohesion: it likely centralizes too much of a subsystem."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: Marinescu\u0027s God Class detection strategy (Lanza and Marinescu, Object-Oriented Metrics in Practice, 2006): all three must hold: WMC (the sum of the members\u0027 cyclomatic complexity) is very high, ATFD (access to foreign data: the distinct fields and properties of other types the class uses as a whole, framework types excluded) is above \u0022few\u0022, and TCC (tight class cohesion, Bieman-Kang: the share of visible method pairs that use common state) is below one third, compared as an exact fraction. Rule sets (--threshold god-class.preset=\u003Cid\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.god-class.preset in .skarp.json): lanza-marinescu-2006 (default): WMC at least 47, ATFD over 5, TCC below 1/3; iplasma: WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3; atfd-2: WMC at least 47, ATFD over 2, TCC below 1/3. The values are Java statistics, not a C# calibration."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "god-class: God Class (judged per type)\n\nA complex class that uses other classes\u0027 data and has low cohesion: it likely centralizes too much of a subsystem.\n\nWhat it measures: Marinescu\u0027s God Class detection strategy (Lanza and Marinescu, Object-Oriented Metrics in\nPractice, 2006): all three must hold: WMC (the sum of the members\u0027 cyclomatic complexity) is very high, ATFD\n(access to foreign data: the distinct fields and properties of other types the class uses as a whole, framework types excluded) is\nabove \u0022few\u0022, and TCC (tight class cohesion, Bieman-Kang: the share of visible method pairs that use common\nstate) is below one third, compared as an exact fraction. Rule sets (--threshold god-class.preset=\u003Cid\u003E on skarp\nanalyze or baseline, or thresholds.god-class.preset in .skarp.json): lanza-marinescu-2006 (default): WMC at\nleast 47, ATFD over 5, TCC below 1/3; iplasma: WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3; atfd-2: WMC at least 47,\nATFD over 2, TCC below 1/3. The values are Java statistics, not a C# calibration. Classes, structs and records\nonly; a type whose TCC is not defined (fewer than two visible methods, no state use) or that was not evaluated\ngets no finding.\nNotice level only. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nWhy it matters: such a class tends to do the work of several, reaching into the data of smaller classes\ninstead of letting them do their part. It is where changes collide and where defects were found more often in\nstudies of Java systems.\n\nHow to improve: find groups of methods that use the same fields (low TCC means there are several) and extract\nthem into their own classes; move logic that mostly works on another class\u0027s data into that class (the foreign\ndata ATFD lists in the semantic JSON names the candidates).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Lanza and Marinescu 2006: Java values, via PMD GodClassRule; the rule set is chosen by the preset parameter)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: preset=lanza-marinescu-2006\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nRule sets (--threshold god-class.preset=\u003Cid\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.god-class.preset in .skarp.json; all must hold; notice only):\n  lanza-marinescu-2006  WMC at least 47, ATFD over 5, TCC below 1/3 (default)  (Lanza and Marinescu 2006: Java values, via PMD GodClassRule)\n  iplasma               WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3  (iPlasma God Class: WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3)\n  atfd-2                WMC at least 47, ATFD over 2, TCC below 1/3  (Lanza and Marinescu 2006 with FEW at 2: the ATFD over 2 variant, also used by Clean-CaDET)\nSwitch off: --rule god-class=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022god-class\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Sources: Lanza and Marinescu 2006 (Object-Oriented Metrics in Practice), Marinescu\u0027s detection strategies, PMD GodClassRule.",
                "markdown": "**god-class: God Class (judged per type)**\n\nA complex class that uses other classes\u0027 data and has low cohesion: it likely centralizes too much of a subsystem.\n\nWhat it measures: Marinescu\u0027s God Class detection strategy (Lanza and Marinescu, Object-Oriented Metrics in Practice, 2006): all three must hold: WMC (the sum of the members\u0027 cyclomatic complexity) is very high, ATFD (access to foreign data: the distinct fields and properties of other types the class uses as a whole, framework types excluded) is above \u0022few\u0022, and TCC (tight class cohesion, Bieman-Kang: the share of visible method pairs that use common state) is below one third, compared as an exact fraction. Rule sets (--threshold god-class.preset=\u003Cid\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.god-class.preset in .skarp.json): lanza-marinescu-2006 (default): WMC at least 47, ATFD over 5, TCC below 1/3; iplasma: WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3; atfd-2: WMC at least 47, ATFD over 2, TCC below 1/3. The values are Java statistics, not a C# calibration. Classes, structs and records only; a type whose TCC is not defined (fewer than two visible methods, no state use) or that was not evaluated gets no finding. Notice level only. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nWhy it matters: such a class tends to do the work of several, reaching into the data of smaller classes instead of letting them do their part. It is where changes collide and where defects were found more often in studies of Java systems.\n\nHow to improve: find groups of methods that use the same fields (low TCC means there are several) and extract them into their own classes; move logic that mostly works on another class\u0027s data into that class (the foreign data ATFD lists in the semantic JSON names the candidates).\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Lanza and Marinescu 2006: Java values, via PMD GodClassRule; the rule set is chosen by the preset parameter)\n    warning: blank\n    parameters: preset=lanza-marinescu-2006\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nRule sets (--threshold god-class.preset=\u003Cid\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.god-class.preset in .skarp.json; all must hold; notice only): lanza-marinescu-2006  WMC at least 47, ATFD over 5, TCC below 1/3 (default)  (Lanza and Marinescu 2006: Java values, via PMD GodClassRule) iplasma               WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3  (iPlasma God Class: WMC over 20, ATFD over 4, TCC below 1/3) atfd-2                WMC at least 47, ATFD over 2, TCC below 1/3  (Lanza and Marinescu 2006 with FEW at 2: the ATFD over 2 variant, also used by Clean-CaDET) Switch off: --rule god-class=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022god-class\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Sources: Lanza and Marinescu 2006 (Object-Oriented Metrics in Practice), Marinescu\u0027s detection strategies, PMD GodClassRule."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "semantic"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "type"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "unused-member",
              "name": "UnusedMember",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A private or internal member that nothing in the solution uses: it can most likely be deleted."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: a private or internal method, constructor with parameters, field, constant, property or event that no code in the solution references, in any analyzed target framework. A reference is any call, object creation, read or write, method group, nameof, attribute argument, operator, pattern call (foreach, await, deconstruction) or signature that binds to it. Members inside an unused type are reported through the type. A field or auto-property that live code writes but never reads is reported as unread-member instead. Only certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never reported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never reported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "unused-member: Unused member (judged per member)\n\nA private or internal member that nothing in the solution uses: it can most likely be deleted.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal method, constructor with parameters, field, constant, property or event\nthat no code in the solution references, in any analyzed target framework. A reference is any call, object\ncreation, read or write, method group, nameof, attribute argument, operator, pattern call (foreach, await,\ndeconstruction) or signature that binds to it. Members inside an unused type are reported through the type.\nA field or auto-property that live code writes but never reads is reported as unread-member instead.\nOnly certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never\nreported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never\nreported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead. Nothing counts as unused when a root\napplies: entry points (Main, top-level statements), overrides, virtual, abstract and extern members, interface\nimplementations, members the compiler calls by pattern (GetEnumerator, Dispose, GetAwaiter, Deconstruct, Add of\na collection, indexers and others), operators, conversions, static constructors, finalizers, partial members,\nevent handlers (object, EventArgs), deserialization constructors and ShouldSerializeX/ResetX pairs, anything with\nan attribute on itself or a containing type (except neutral ones such as Obsolete, SuppressMessage, Conditional,\nDebuggerDisplay, MethodImpl and the nullable annotations), types derived from framework-scanned bases (ASP.NET\ncontrollers, hosted services, MediatR handlers, EF migrations and others), members of a type used in typeof(T)\nor with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], fields of structs with native layout use, a name that appears in a string\nliteral, dynamic or invalid code or inactive #if code, InternalsVisibleTo to an assembly outside the solution (all\ninternal members of that assembly), and the allowlist (unusedCode.allow in .skarp.json: documentation ids such as\nM:Ns.Type.Member or names such as Ns.Type.Member, with * and ?). A type is kept when one of its extension\nmethods is kept by its name. A use inside the member itself (recursion) or inside the type itself does not count;\na documentation cref does not count.\n\nCode used only by unused code: uses count only when they come from live code: public and\nprotected code, generated code, roots, code that is not evaluated, and whatever live code uses in turn. Code\nthat only unused code uses is reported too, with \u0022used only by unused code: X (and N more)\u0022, and members that\ncall each other but nothing else uses are reported together. Overrides, interface implementations and pattern\nmembers inside a type nothing uses keep nothing alive; framework-scanned types, attributes and names stay roots.\nThe semantic JSON gives usedOnlyBy (the unused users) and depth (0: nothing references it; 1: only code at\ndepth 0 does; and so on). Delete from depth 0 down; the deeper findings disappear with their users.\n\nMulti-targeted projects: by default only the highest target framework is analyzed and unused code is\nreported from it; the finding says so, for example \u0022(net10.0 only; --all-targets for all)\u0022. Uses that only other\ntargets have count through names: names in inactive #if code, names in files that only other targets compile\n(conditional Compile items), and, for an extension method in a file another target compiles, a call in the\nanalyzed target to a framework method of the same name (another target may bind it to the extension, as with\npolyfills). With --all-targets on skarp analyze or baseline every target is analyzed and the\nresult is certain.\n\nNot evaluated (no finding, counted in the report): types with unresolved symbols or invalid code (their\nreferences still count), projects with XAML or Razor files whose generated C# is missing, projects with source\ngenerators under --no-generators, and internal members visible to a friend project that gave no compiler\narguments or was not analyzed (--target on skarp analyze or baseline).\n\nWhy it matters: dead code still costs reading, reviewing, migrating and testing time, and it misleads readers about\nwhat the program does.\n\nHow to improve: delete it, together with the code only it uses (the findings that name it in usedOnlyBy). If it\nis used through a route skarp cannot see (reflection by computed names, external callers), add an attribute such\nas [UsedImplicitly] or an allowlist entry. Notice level; new findings trip the gate only when it is configured\nfor them (--gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json); code used\nonly by unused code counts as unused too, so a gate on notices can show new findings in code that did not change. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: certain unused code only, after Roslyn IDE0051 widened to internal members and types)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nSwitch off: --rule unused-member=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022unused-member\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Gate: --gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [\u0022unused-member:notice\u0022] } in .skarp.json.\nSources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule.",
                "markdown": "**unused-member: Unused member (judged per member)**\n\nA private or internal member that nothing in the solution uses: it can most likely be deleted.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal method, constructor with parameters, field, constant, property or event that no code in the solution references, in any analyzed target framework. A reference is any call, object creation, read or write, method group, nameof, attribute argument, operator, pattern call (foreach, await, deconstruction) or signature that binds to it. Members inside an unused type are reported through the type. A field or auto-property that live code writes but never reads is reported as unread-member instead. Only certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never reported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never reported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead. Nothing counts as unused when a root applies: entry points (Main, top-level statements), overrides, virtual, abstract and extern members, interface implementations, members the compiler calls by pattern (GetEnumerator, Dispose, GetAwaiter, Deconstruct, Add of a collection, indexers and others), operators, conversions, static constructors, finalizers, partial members, event handlers (object, EventArgs), deserialization constructors and ShouldSerializeX/ResetX pairs, anything with an attribute on itself or a containing type (except neutral ones such as Obsolete, SuppressMessage, Conditional, DebuggerDisplay, MethodImpl and the nullable annotations), types derived from framework-scanned bases (ASP.NET controllers, hosted services, MediatR handlers, EF migrations and others), members of a type used in typeof(T) or with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], fields of structs with native layout use, a name that appears in a string literal, dynamic or invalid code or inactive #if code, InternalsVisibleTo to an assembly outside the solution (all internal members of that assembly), and the allowlist (unusedCode.allow in .skarp.json: documentation ids such as M:Ns.Type.Member or names such as Ns.Type.Member, with * and ?). A type is kept when one of its extension methods is kept by its name. A use inside the member itself (recursion) or inside the type itself does not count; a documentation cref does not count.\n\nCode used only by unused code: uses count only when they come from live code: public and protected code, generated code, roots, code that is not evaluated, and whatever live code uses in turn. Code that only unused code uses is reported too, with \u0022used only by unused code: X (and N more)\u0022, and members that call each other but nothing else uses are reported together. Overrides, interface implementations and pattern members inside a type nothing uses keep nothing alive; framework-scanned types, attributes and names stay roots. The semantic JSON gives usedOnlyBy (the unused users) and depth (0: nothing references it; 1: only code at depth 0 does; and so on). Delete from depth 0 down; the deeper findings disappear with their users.\n\nMulti-targeted projects: by default only the highest target framework is analyzed and unused code is reported from it; the finding says so, for example \u0022(net10.0 only; --all-targets for all)\u0022. Uses that only other targets have count through names: names in inactive #if code, names in files that only other targets compile (conditional Compile items), and, for an extension method in a file another target compiles, a call in the analyzed target to a framework method of the same name (another target may bind it to the extension, as with polyfills). With --all-targets on skarp analyze or baseline every target is analyzed and the result is certain.\n\nNot evaluated (no finding, counted in the report): types with unresolved symbols or invalid code (their references still count), projects with XAML or Razor files whose generated C# is missing, projects with source generators under --no-generators, and internal members visible to a friend project that gave no compiler arguments or was not analyzed (--target on skarp analyze or baseline).\n\nWhy it matters: dead code still costs reading, reviewing, migrating and testing time, and it misleads readers about what the program does.\n\nHow to improve: delete it, together with the code only it uses (the findings that name it in usedOnlyBy). If it is used through a route skarp cannot see (reflection by computed names, external callers), add an attribute such as [UsedImplicitly] or an allowlist entry. Notice level; new findings trip the gate only when it is configured for them (--gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json); code used only by unused code counts as unused too, so a gate on notices can show new findings in code that did not change. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: certain unused code only, after Roslyn IDE0051 widened to internal members and types)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nSwitch off: --rule unused-member=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022unused-member\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Gate: --gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [\u0022unused-member:notice\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Sources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "semantic"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "unused-type",
              "name": "UnusedType",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A private or internal type that nothing in the solution uses: it can most likely be deleted with its members."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: a private or internal class, struct, record, interface, enum or delegate that no code in the solution references from outside the type itself: no creation, static member access, base list, signature, cast, pattern, typeof, generic argument or attribute use. A type that implements an interface or derives from a class is reported too, unless the base is a framework-scanned one: dependency-injection containers that register implementations by scanning assemblies are invisible to skarp, so check before deleting. Only certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never reported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never reported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "unused-type: Unused type (judged per type)\n\nA private or internal type that nothing in the solution uses: it can most likely be deleted with its members.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal class, struct, record, interface, enum or delegate that no code in the\nsolution references from outside the type itself: no creation, static member access, base list, signature,\ncast, pattern, typeof, generic argument or attribute use. A type that implements an interface or derives from a\nclass is reported too, unless the base is a framework-scanned one: dependency-injection containers\nthat register implementations by scanning assemblies are invisible to skarp, so check before deleting.\nOnly certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never\nreported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never\nreported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead. Nothing counts as unused when a root\napplies: entry points (Main, top-level statements), overrides, virtual, abstract and extern members, interface\nimplementations, members the compiler calls by pattern (GetEnumerator, Dispose, GetAwaiter, Deconstruct, Add of\na collection, indexers and others), operators, conversions, static constructors, finalizers, partial members,\nevent handlers (object, EventArgs), deserialization constructors and ShouldSerializeX/ResetX pairs, anything with\nan attribute on itself or a containing type (except neutral ones such as Obsolete, SuppressMessage, Conditional,\nDebuggerDisplay, MethodImpl and the nullable annotations), types derived from framework-scanned bases (ASP.NET\ncontrollers, hosted services, MediatR handlers, EF migrations and others), members of a type used in typeof(T)\nor with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], fields of structs with native layout use, a name that appears in a string\nliteral, dynamic or invalid code or inactive #if code, InternalsVisibleTo to an assembly outside the solution (all\ninternal members of that assembly), and the allowlist (unusedCode.allow in .skarp.json: documentation ids such as\nM:Ns.Type.Member or names such as Ns.Type.Member, with * and ?). A type is kept when one of its extension\nmethods is kept by its name. A use inside the member itself (recursion) or inside the type itself does not count;\na documentation cref does not count.\n\nCode used only by unused code: uses count only when they come from live code: public and\nprotected code, generated code, roots, code that is not evaluated, and whatever live code uses in turn. Code\nthat only unused code uses is reported too, with \u0022used only by unused code: X (and N more)\u0022, and members that\ncall each other but nothing else uses are reported together. Overrides, interface implementations and pattern\nmembers inside a type nothing uses keep nothing alive; framework-scanned types, attributes and names stay roots.\nThe semantic JSON gives usedOnlyBy (the unused users) and depth (0: nothing references it; 1: only code at\ndepth 0 does; and so on). Delete from depth 0 down; the deeper findings disappear with their users.\n\nMulti-targeted projects: by default only the highest target framework is analyzed and unused code is\nreported from it; the finding says so, for example \u0022(net10.0 only; --all-targets for all)\u0022. Uses that only other\ntargets have count through names: names in inactive #if code, names in files that only other targets compile\n(conditional Compile items), and, for an extension method in a file another target compiles, a call in the\nanalyzed target to a framework method of the same name (another target may bind it to the extension, as with\npolyfills). With --all-targets on skarp analyze or baseline every target is analyzed and the\nresult is certain.\n\nNot evaluated (no finding, counted in the report): types with unresolved symbols or invalid code (their\nreferences still count), projects with XAML or Razor files whose generated C# is missing, projects with source\ngenerators under --no-generators, and internal members visible to a friend project that gave no compiler\narguments or was not analyzed (--target on skarp analyze or baseline).\n\nWhy it matters: dead code still costs reading, reviewing, migrating and testing time, and it misleads readers about\nwhat the program does.\n\nHow to improve: delete it, together with the code only it uses (the findings that name it in usedOnlyBy). If it\nis used through a route skarp cannot see (reflection by computed names, external callers), add an attribute such\nas [UsedImplicitly] or an allowlist entry. Notice level; new findings trip the gate only when it is configured\nfor them (--gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json); code used\nonly by unused code counts as unused too, so a gate on notices can show new findings in code that did not change. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: certain unused code only, after Roslyn IDE0051 widened to internal members and types)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nSwitch off: --rule unused-type=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022unused-type\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Gate: --gate-on unused-type:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [\u0022unused-type:notice\u0022] } in .skarp.json.\nSources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule.",
                "markdown": "**unused-type: Unused type (judged per type)**\n\nA private or internal type that nothing in the solution uses: it can most likely be deleted with its members.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal class, struct, record, interface, enum or delegate that no code in the solution references from outside the type itself: no creation, static member access, base list, signature, cast, pattern, typeof, generic argument or attribute use. A type that implements an interface or derives from a class is reported too, unless the base is a framework-scanned one: dependency-injection containers that register implementations by scanning assemblies are invisible to skarp, so check before deleting. Only certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never reported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never reported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead. Nothing counts as unused when a root applies: entry points (Main, top-level statements), overrides, virtual, abstract and extern members, interface implementations, members the compiler calls by pattern (GetEnumerator, Dispose, GetAwaiter, Deconstruct, Add of a collection, indexers and others), operators, conversions, static constructors, finalizers, partial members, event handlers (object, EventArgs), deserialization constructors and ShouldSerializeX/ResetX pairs, anything with an attribute on itself or a containing type (except neutral ones such as Obsolete, SuppressMessage, Conditional, DebuggerDisplay, MethodImpl and the nullable annotations), types derived from framework-scanned bases (ASP.NET controllers, hosted services, MediatR handlers, EF migrations and others), members of a type used in typeof(T) or with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], fields of structs with native layout use, a name that appears in a string literal, dynamic or invalid code or inactive #if code, InternalsVisibleTo to an assembly outside the solution (all internal members of that assembly), and the allowlist (unusedCode.allow in .skarp.json: documentation ids such as M:Ns.Type.Member or names such as Ns.Type.Member, with * and ?). A type is kept when one of its extension methods is kept by its name. A use inside the member itself (recursion) or inside the type itself does not count; a documentation cref does not count.\n\nCode used only by unused code: uses count only when they come from live code: public and protected code, generated code, roots, code that is not evaluated, and whatever live code uses in turn. Code that only unused code uses is reported too, with \u0022used only by unused code: X (and N more)\u0022, and members that call each other but nothing else uses are reported together. Overrides, interface implementations and pattern members inside a type nothing uses keep nothing alive; framework-scanned types, attributes and names stay roots. The semantic JSON gives usedOnlyBy (the unused users) and depth (0: nothing references it; 1: only code at depth 0 does; and so on). Delete from depth 0 down; the deeper findings disappear with their users.\n\nMulti-targeted projects: by default only the highest target framework is analyzed and unused code is reported from it; the finding says so, for example \u0022(net10.0 only; --all-targets for all)\u0022. Uses that only other targets have count through names: names in inactive #if code, names in files that only other targets compile (conditional Compile items), and, for an extension method in a file another target compiles, a call in the analyzed target to a framework method of the same name (another target may bind it to the extension, as with polyfills). With --all-targets on skarp analyze or baseline every target is analyzed and the result is certain.\n\nNot evaluated (no finding, counted in the report): types with unresolved symbols or invalid code (their references still count), projects with XAML or Razor files whose generated C# is missing, projects with source generators under --no-generators, and internal members visible to a friend project that gave no compiler arguments or was not analyzed (--target on skarp analyze or baseline).\n\nWhy it matters: dead code still costs reading, reviewing, migrating and testing time, and it misleads readers about what the program does.\n\nHow to improve: delete it, together with the code only it uses (the findings that name it in usedOnlyBy). If it is used through a route skarp cannot see (reflection by computed names, external callers), add an attribute such as [UsedImplicitly] or an allowlist entry. Notice level; new findings trip the gate only when it is configured for them (--gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json); code used only by unused code counts as unused too, so a gate on notices can show new findings in code that did not change. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: certain unused code only, after Roslyn IDE0051 widened to internal members and types)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nSwitch off: --rule unused-type=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022unused-type\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Gate: --gate-on unused-type:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [\u0022unused-type:notice\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Sources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule."
              },
