            {
              "id": "used-only-by-tests",
              "name": "UsedOnlyByTests",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Production code that only test projects use: a label, never gated; the code may be dead apart from its tests."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: a private or internal member or type of a production project that only test projects (or files using a test framework) reference, directly or through other code only tests use (\u0022used only by tests (via X)\u0022). Also a field or auto-property production code writes and only tests read, directly or through such code (\u0022written by production code but read only by tests\u0022). It is a label, not a defect: the code may be a test hook, or it may be dead production code that only its tests keep alive. It is never gated (--gate-on cannot select it). Only certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never reported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never reported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "used-only-by-tests: Used only by tests (judged per member)\n\nProduction code that only test projects use: a label, never gated; the code may be dead apart from its tests.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal member or type of a production project that only test projects (or\nfiles using a test framework) reference, directly or through other code only tests use (\u0022used only by tests\n(via X)\u0022). Also a field or auto-property production code writes and only tests read, directly or through\nsuch code (\u0022written by production code but read only by tests\u0022). It is a label, not a defect: the code may be a test hook, or it may be dead production code that\nonly its tests keep alive. It is never gated (--gate-on cannot select it).\nOnly certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never\nreported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never\nreported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead. Nothing counts as unused when a root\napplies: entry points (Main, top-level statements), overrides, virtual, abstract and extern members, interface\nimplementations, members the compiler calls by pattern (GetEnumerator, Dispose, GetAwaiter, Deconstruct, Add of\na collection, indexers and others), operators, conversions, static constructors, finalizers, partial members,\nevent handlers (object, EventArgs), deserialization constructors and ShouldSerializeX/ResetX pairs, anything with\nan attribute on itself or a containing type (except neutral ones such as Obsolete, SuppressMessage, Conditional,\nDebuggerDisplay, MethodImpl and the nullable annotations), types derived from framework-scanned bases (ASP.NET\ncontrollers, hosted services, MediatR handlers, EF migrations and others), members of a type used in typeof(T)\nor with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], fields of structs with native layout use, a name that appears in a string\nliteral, dynamic or invalid code or inactive #if code, InternalsVisibleTo to an assembly outside the solution (all\ninternal members of that assembly), and the allowlist (unusedCode.allow in .skarp.json: documentation ids such as\nM:Ns.Type.Member or names such as Ns.Type.Member, with * and ?). A type is kept when one of its extension\nmethods is kept by its name. A use inside the member itself (recursion) or inside the type itself does not count;\na documentation cref does not count.\n\nCode used only by unused code: uses count only when they come from live code: public and\nprotected code, generated code, roots, code that is not evaluated, and whatever live code uses in turn. Code\nthat only unused code uses is reported too, with \u0022used only by unused code: X (and N more)\u0022, and members that\ncall each other but nothing else uses are reported together. Overrides, interface implementations and pattern\nmembers inside a type nothing uses keep nothing alive; framework-scanned types, attributes and names stay roots.\nThe semantic JSON gives usedOnlyBy (the unused users) and depth (0: nothing references it; 1: only code at\ndepth 0 does; and so on). Delete from depth 0 down; the deeper findings disappear with their users.\n\nMulti-targeted projects: by default only the highest target framework is analyzed and unused code is\nreported from it; the finding says so, for example \u0022(net10.0 only; --all-targets for all)\u0022. Uses that only other\ntargets have count through names: names in inactive #if code, names in files that only other targets compile\n(conditional Compile items), and, for an extension method in a file another target compiles, a call in the\nanalyzed target to a framework method of the same name (another target may bind it to the extension, as with\npolyfills). With --all-targets on skarp analyze or baseline every target is analyzed and the\nresult is certain.\n\nNot evaluated (no finding, counted in the report): types with unresolved symbols or invalid code (their\nreferences still count), projects with XAML or Razor files whose generated C# is missing, projects with source\ngenerators under --no-generators, and internal members visible to a friend project that gave no compiler\narguments or was not analyzed (--target on skarp analyze or baseline).\n\nWhy it matters: dead code still costs reading, reviewing, migrating and testing time, and it misleads readers about\nwhat the program does.\n\nHow to improve: delete it, together with the code only it uses (the findings that name it in usedOnlyBy). If it\nis used through a route skarp cannot see (reflection by computed names, external callers), add an attribute such\nas [UsedImplicitly] or an allowlist entry. Notice level; new findings trip the gate only when it is configured\nfor them (--gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json); code used\nonly by unused code counts as unused too, so a gate on notices can show new findings in code that did not change. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own, after NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule; a label, never gated)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nSwitch off: --rule used-only-by-tests=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022used-only-by-tests\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Never gated.\nSources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule.",
                "markdown": "**used-only-by-tests: Used only by tests (judged per member)**\n\nProduction code that only test projects use: a label, never gated; the code may be dead apart from its tests.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal member or type of a production project that only test projects (or files using a test framework) reference, directly or through other code only tests use (\u0022used only by tests (via X)\u0022). Also a field or auto-property production code writes and only tests read, directly or through such code (\u0022written by production code but read only by tests\u0022). It is a label, not a defect: the code may be a test hook, or it may be dead production code that only its tests keep alive. It is never gated (--gate-on cannot select it). Only certain findings: private and internal members and types; public and protected ones are never reported (code outside the solution may use them). Test projects are compiled and walked as users but never reported; code they alone use gets the label used-only-by-tests instead. Nothing counts as unused when a root applies: entry points (Main, top-level statements), overrides, virtual, abstract and extern members, interface implementations, members the compiler calls by pattern (GetEnumerator, Dispose, GetAwaiter, Deconstruct, Add of a collection, indexers and others), operators, conversions, static constructors, finalizers, partial members, event handlers (object, EventArgs), deserialization constructors and ShouldSerializeX/ResetX pairs, anything with an attribute on itself or a containing type (except neutral ones such as Obsolete, SuppressMessage, Conditional, DebuggerDisplay, MethodImpl and the nullable annotations), types derived from framework-scanned bases (ASP.NET controllers, hosted services, MediatR handlers, EF migrations and others), members of a type used in typeof(T) or with [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], fields of structs with native layout use, a name that appears in a string literal, dynamic or invalid code or inactive #if code, InternalsVisibleTo to an assembly outside the solution (all internal members of that assembly), and the allowlist (unusedCode.allow in .skarp.json: documentation ids such as M:Ns.Type.Member or names such as Ns.Type.Member, with * and ?). A type is kept when one of its extension methods is kept by its name. A use inside the member itself (recursion) or inside the type itself does not count; a documentation cref does not count.\n\nCode used only by unused code: uses count only when they come from live code: public and protected code, generated code, roots, code that is not evaluated, and whatever live code uses in turn. Code that only unused code uses is reported too, with \u0022used only by unused code: X (and N more)\u0022, and members that call each other but nothing else uses are reported together. Overrides, interface implementations and pattern members inside a type nothing uses keep nothing alive; framework-scanned types, attributes and names stay roots. The semantic JSON gives usedOnlyBy (the unused users) and depth (0: nothing references it; 1: only code at depth 0 does; and so on). Delete from depth 0 down; the deeper findings disappear with their users.\n\nMulti-targeted projects: by default only the highest target framework is analyzed and unused code is reported from it; the finding says so, for example \u0022(net10.0 only; --all-targets for all)\u0022. Uses that only other targets have count through names: names in inactive #if code, names in files that only other targets compile (conditional Compile items), and, for an extension method in a file another target compiles, a call in the analyzed target to a framework method of the same name (another target may bind it to the extension, as with polyfills). With --all-targets on skarp analyze or baseline every target is analyzed and the result is certain.\n\nNot evaluated (no finding, counted in the report): types with unresolved symbols or invalid code (their references still count), projects with XAML or Razor files whose generated C# is missing, projects with source generators under --no-generators, and internal members visible to a friend project that gave no compiler arguments or was not analyzed (--target on skarp analyze or baseline).\n\nWhy it matters: dead code still costs reading, reviewing, migrating and testing time, and it misleads readers about what the program does.\n\nHow to improve: delete it, together with the code only it uses (the findings that name it in usedOnlyBy). If it is used through a route skarp cannot see (reflection by computed names, external callers), add an attribute such as [UsedImplicitly] or an allowlist entry. Notice level; new findings trip the gate only when it is configured for them (--gate-on unused-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json); code used only by unused code counts as unused too, so a gate on notices can show new findings in code that did not change. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own, after NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule; a label, never gated)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nSwitch off: --rule used-only-by-tests=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022used-only-by-tests\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Never gated. Sources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "semantic"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "unread-member",
              "name": "UnreadMember",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A private or internal field or auto-property that is written but never read: check its writes for side effects, then remove it."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: a private or internal field or auto-property that live code writes but never reads, in any analyzed target framework. Writes: assignments (also in object initializers and with expressions), a non-constant initializer, out arguments, deconstruction targets, and compound assignments or \u002B\u002B and -- whose value is not used. Everything else reads: ref and in arguments (Interlocked, Volatile), nameof, ??=, address-of, a member access through it (_list.Add(x), _struct.X = 1). Reads from unused code do not count; a member only tests read gets the used-only-by-tests label instead. After Roslyn IDE0052, widened to internal members."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "unread-member: Unread member (judged per member)\n\nA private or internal field or auto-property that is written but never read: check its writes for side effects, then remove it.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal field or auto-property that live code writes but never\nreads, in any analyzed target framework. Writes: assignments (also in object initializers and with\nexpressions), a non-constant initializer, out arguments, deconstruction targets, and compound assignments or\n\u002B\u002B and -- whose value is not used. Everything else reads: ref and in arguments (Interlocked, Volatile), nameof,\n??=, address-of, a member access through it (_list.Add(x), _struct.X = 1). Reads from unused code do not count;\na member only tests read gets the used-only-by-tests label instead. After Roslyn IDE0052, widened to\ninternal members.\n\nCertain only: not reported are properties with bodies, members of records (their synthesized Equals and ToString\nread them), instance members of structs, members of [StructLayout] or [InlineArray] types, and fields or\nproperties of delegate, IDisposable, GCHandle, object or dynamic type, or of a type parameter that can be any\ntype (no constraint type, not struct or unmanaged), as writing them keeps an object alive; the summary lists\nthem as rooted with that reason. The roots of unused-member apply (attributes, names in strings, typeof and\n[DynamicallyAccessedMembers], a type passed to a [RequiresUnreferencedCode] method such as a reflection-based\nserializer, InternalsVisibleTo outside the solution, the allowlist).\n\nWhy it matters: a value nobody reads is dead state: it costs memory, readers look for its purpose, and the code\ncomputing it may be dead too.\n\nHow to improve: check each write for side effects (a call that computes the value, an object kept alive on\npurpose), then remove the member and its writes; keep the side effects that matter. If something reads it\nthrough a route skarp cannot see, add an attribute or an allowlist entry (unusedCode.allow). Notice level;\ngateable with --gate-on unread-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in\n.skarp.json. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: certain written-but-never-read members only, after Roslyn IDE0052 widened to internal members)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nSwitch off: --rule unread-member=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022unread-member\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Gate: --gate-on unread-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [\u0022unread-member:notice\u0022] } in .skarp.json.\nSources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule.",
                "markdown": "**unread-member: Unread member (judged per member)**\n\nA private or internal field or auto-property that is written but never read: check its writes for side effects, then remove it.\n\nWhat it measures: a private or internal field or auto-property that live code writes but never reads, in any analyzed target framework. Writes: assignments (also in object initializers and with expressions), a non-constant initializer, out arguments, deconstruction targets, and compound assignments or \u002B\u002B and -- whose value is not used. Everything else reads: ref and in arguments (Interlocked, Volatile), nameof, ??=, address-of, a member access through it (_list.Add(x), _struct.X = 1). Reads from unused code do not count; a member only tests read gets the used-only-by-tests label instead. After Roslyn IDE0052, widened to internal members.\n\nCertain only: not reported are properties with bodies, members of records (their synthesized Equals and ToString read them), instance members of structs, members of [StructLayout] or [InlineArray] types, and fields or properties of delegate, IDisposable, GCHandle, object or dynamic type, or of a type parameter that can be any type (no constraint type, not struct or unmanaged), as writing them keeps an object alive; the summary lists them as rooted with that reason. The roots of unused-member apply (attributes, names in strings, typeof and [DynamicallyAccessedMembers], a type passed to a [RequiresUnreferencedCode] method such as a reflection-based serializer, InternalsVisibleTo outside the solution, the allowlist).\n\nWhy it matters: a value nobody reads is dead state: it costs memory, readers look for its purpose, and the code computing it may be dead too.\n\nHow to improve: check each write for side effects (a call that computes the value, an object kept alive on purpose), then remove the member and its writes; keep the side effects that matter. If something reads it through a route skarp cannot see, add an attribute or an allowlist entry (unusedCode.allow). Notice level; gateable with --gate-on unread-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [...] } in .skarp.json. Needs the semantic tier.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    notice:  structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: certain written-but-never-read members only, after Roslyn IDE0052 widened to internal members)\n    warning: blank\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nSwitch off: --rule unread-member=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022unread-member\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Allowlist: \u0022unusedCode\u0022: { \u0022allow\u0022: [\u0022M:Ns.Type.Member\u0022, \u0022Ns.Type.*\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Gate: --gate-on unread-member:notice on skarp analyze, or \u0022gate\u0022: { \u0022on\u0022: [\u0022unread-member:notice\u0022] } in .skarp.json. Sources: Roslyn IDE0051 and IDE0052 (unused and unread private members), NDepend\u0027s \u0022code used only by tests\u0022 rule."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "semantic"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "namespace-cycle",
              "name": "NamespaceCycle",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Namespaces that depend on each other in a cycle cannot be understood, tested or moved apart; cut the cheapest dependency first."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: the namespace dependency graph of the solution\u0027s non-test projects: a namespace depends on another when a type declared in it uses a type declared in the other (base types, signatures, attributes, members called or read, object creation, casts, patterns, typeof and nameof; using directives and doc-comment crefs do not count). Nodes are (assembly, namespace): a namespace name declared in several assemblies is one node per assembly and is named with the assembly in parentheses; parent and child namespaces are separate nodes. Sources are the kept, handwritten, non-test files; references to framework and package types do not count, nor (by default) references to types declared only in generated code (--include-generated-targets on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022namespaceCycles\u0022: { \u0022includeGeneratedTargets\u0022: true } in .skarp.json, keeps them; experimental). The weight of a dependency is its number of distinct (using type, used type) pairs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "namespace-cycle: Namespace cycle (judged per namespace)\n\nNamespaces that depend on each other in a cycle cannot be understood, tested or moved apart; cut the cheapest dependency first.\n\nWhat it measures: the namespace dependency graph of the solution\u0027s non-test projects: a namespace depends on\nanother when a type declared in it uses a type declared in the other (base types, signatures, attributes,\nmembers called or read, object creation, casts, patterns, typeof and nameof; using directives and doc-comment\ncrefs do not count). Nodes are (assembly, namespace): a namespace name declared in several assemblies is one node\nper assembly and is named with the assembly in parentheses; parent and child namespaces are separate nodes.\nSources are the kept, handwritten, non-test files; references to framework and package types do not count, nor\n(by default) references to types declared only in generated code (--include-generated-targets on skarp analyze\nor baseline, or \u0022namespaceCycles\u0022: { \u0022includeGeneratedTargets\u0022: true } in .skarp.json, keeps them; experimental). The weight of a dependency is its number of distinct (using type, used type) pairs. One finding\nper strongly connected component of two or more namespaces (Tarjan\u0027s algorithm), named after its ordinal-first\nnamespace; its value is the number of namespaces (a growing cycle is worsened). The message leads with the\ncheapest cuts: a suggested set of dependencies whose removal breaks every cycle (greedy: repeatedly the lightest\ndependency of the shortest remaining cycle; a suggestion, not a guaranteed minimum), then the members and one\nshortest cycle; the finding points at the first reference of the cheapest cut. The semantic JSON lists, per\ncomponent, the mutual pairs (lighter direction first), every dependency with up to 5 example references\n(path:line, using type, used type) and the cut. Needs the semantic tier. Warning level.\n\nWhy it matters: namespaces in a cycle form one unit: none can be understood, tested, reused or moved to its own\nassembly without the others, and a change in one can ripple around the cycle (the Acyclic Dependencies\nPrinciple, Robert C. Martin; levelization, John Lakos). Tools such as NDepend, Structure101 and Lattix report\nthem for the same reason. Large cycles usually hide a few light back references.\n\nHow to improve: start with the cheapest cut: open its example references; usually a constant, a helper, an\ninterface or a type in the wrong namespace makes a lower-level namespace depend on a higher one. Move that type,\ninvert the dependency with an interface or event owned by the lower namespace, or pass the value in instead of\nlooking it up. Re-run skarp: the component shrinks or disappears.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n  default\n    warning: structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: every cycle, after NDepend ND1400/ND1401 and Structure101\u0027s 0% tangle default)\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\nSwitch off: --rule namespace-cycle=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022namespace-cycle\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Keep references to types declared only in generated code: --include-generated-targets on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022namespaceCycles\u0022: { \u0022includeGeneratedTargets\u0022: true } in .skarp.json (experimental). The finding id has no path: namespace-cycle||namespace \u003Cfirst member\u003E.\nSources: Tarjan 1972 (strongly connected components); NDepend, Structure101, Lattix, ArchUnit and import-linter (namespace and package cycles).",
                "markdown": "**namespace-cycle: Namespace cycle (judged per namespace)**\n\nNamespaces that depend on each other in a cycle cannot be understood, tested or moved apart; cut the cheapest dependency first.\n\nWhat it measures: the namespace dependency graph of the solution\u0027s non-test projects: a namespace depends on another when a type declared in it uses a type declared in the other (base types, signatures, attributes, members called or read, object creation, casts, patterns, typeof and nameof; using directives and doc-comment crefs do not count). Nodes are (assembly, namespace): a namespace name declared in several assemblies is one node per assembly and is named with the assembly in parentheses; parent and child namespaces are separate nodes. Sources are the kept, handwritten, non-test files; references to framework and package types do not count, nor (by default) references to types declared only in generated code (--include-generated-targets on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022namespaceCycles\u0022: { \u0022includeGeneratedTargets\u0022: true } in .skarp.json, keeps them; experimental). The weight of a dependency is its number of distinct (using type, used type) pairs. One finding per strongly connected component of two or more namespaces (Tarjan\u0027s algorithm), named after its ordinal-first namespace; its value is the number of namespaces (a growing cycle is worsened). The message leads with the cheapest cuts: a suggested set of dependencies whose removal breaks every cycle (greedy: repeatedly the lightest dependency of the shortest remaining cycle; a suggestion, not a guaranteed minimum), then the members and one shortest cycle; the finding points at the first reference of the cheapest cut. The semantic JSON lists, per component, the mutual pairs (lighter direction first), every dependency with up to 5 example references (path:line, using type, used type) and the cut. Needs the semantic tier. Warning level.\n\nWhy it matters: namespaces in a cycle form one unit: none can be understood, tested, reused or moved to its own assembly without the others, and a change in one can ripple around the cycle (the Acyclic Dependencies Principle, Robert C. Martin; levelization, John Lakos). Tools such as NDepend, Structure101 and Lattix report them for the same reason. Large cycles usually hide a few light back references.\n\nHow to improve: start with the cheapest cut: open its example references; usually a constant, a helper, an interface or a type in the wrong namespace makes a lower-level namespace depend on a higher one. Move that type, invert the dependency with an interface or event owned by the lower namespace, or pass the value in instead of looking it up. Re-run skarp: the component shrinks or disappears.\n\nThresholds per preset (\u0022over\u0022 = greater than; \u0022at least\u0022 = from the value on; a blank warning means notice only):\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060text\n  default\n    warning: structural  (Skarp\u0027s own: every cycle, after NDepend ND1400/ND1401 and Structure101\u0027s 0% tangle default)\n  codescene: no value (the default is used)\n  clean-cadet: no value (the default is used)\n  doomen: no value (the default is used)\n  reportgenerator: no value (the default is used)\n  ndepend: no value (the default is used)\n  pmd: no value (the default is used)\n  checkstyle: no value (the default is used)\n  microsoft: no value (the default is used)\n  jscpd: no value (the default is used)\n  alves-percentile: no value (the default is used)\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\nSwitch off: --rule namespace-cycle=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022namespace-cycle\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Keep references to types declared only in generated code: --include-generated-targets on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022namespaceCycles\u0022: { \u0022includeGeneratedTargets\u0022: true } in .skarp.json (experimental). The finding id has no path: namespace-cycle||namespace \u003Cfirst member\u003E. Sources: Tarjan 1972 (strongly connected components); NDepend, Structure101, Lattix, ArchUnit and import-linter (namespace and package cycles)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "semantic"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "warning",
                "unit": "namespace"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "brain-method",
              "name": "BrainMethod",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A member that is long, complex and deeply nested at once, reported as one finding listing those parts; on by default."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "What it measures: a member that has all of the long-method, complex-method and deep-nesting findings at once (each at its own threshold in effect: by default over 50 physical lines, cognitive complexity over 15, nesting depth over 3). It finds nothing the parts do not find: it groups them under one name. The part set is the rule parameter parts (comma-separated member rules: long-method, complex-method, deep-nesting, bumpy-road, complex-condition, too-many-parameters, path-explosion) and minParts (how many of them must fire; default all). Lanza and Marinescu\u0027s Brain Method (2006) adds a fourth leg, many accessed variables; skarp leaves it out at the syntax tier, as CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method (size, complexity, nesting) does."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "brain-method: Brain method (composite) (judged per member)\n\nA member that is long, complex and deeply nested at once, reported as one finding listing those parts; on by default.\n\nWhat it measures: a member that has all of the long-method, complex-method and deep-nesting findings at once\n(each at its own threshold in effect: by default over 50 physical lines, cognitive complexity over 15, nesting\ndepth over 3). It finds nothing the parts do not find: it groups them under one name. The part set is the rule\nparameter parts (comma-separated member rules: long-method, complex-method, deep-nesting, bumpy-road,\ncomplex-condition, too-many-parameters, path-explosion) and minParts (how many of them must fire; default all).\nLanza and Marinescu\u0027s Brain Method (2006) adds a fourth leg, many accessed variables; skarp leaves it out at the\nsyntax tier, as CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method (size, complexity, nesting) does.\n\nWhy it matters: a method that is long, branchy and deeply nested at once tends to hold the logic of its class in\none place (\u0022centralizes the functionality\u0022, Lanza and Marinescu): it is the hardest kind of method to read, test\nand change safely, and it attracts more change.\n\nHow to improve: split it. Extract the commented or blank-line separated steps into methods with names that say\nwhat they do (Extract Method); flatten the nesting with guard clauses, early returns and continue; move each\nbranch of a large if/switch into its own method or a lookup table; pull loop bodies out into methods. Re-run\nskarp: each part that drops below its threshold leaves the composite, and it disappears when a part is gone.\nHow it is reported: one line in place of its parts (the text format, Markdown, GitHub annotations and\nskarp check), listing them; --all on skarp analyze and skarp show \u003Chandle\u003E list the parts one by one. The JSON keeps every part\nfinding in findings and adds the composite to the composites list (rule, place, severity, status, part ids); SARIF\nreports it with its parts as related locations, next to the parts\u0027 own results. The composite has no threshold,\nscore weight or gate entry of its own: the baseline records the parts, the score charges the parts once, and the\ngate judges the parts. Its severity is the highest of its parts; against the baseline it is known when every part\nis known, new when every part is new, and worsened otherwise.\n\nSuppression: [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022brain-method\u0022, Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022)] on the member or type hides the\ncomposite and all its parts there. Suppressing one part hides that part only; the composite stays when the\nremaining findings still meet its definition.\n\nOn by default (CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method is on in its C# rules, NDepend\u0027s \u0022Avoid methods too big, too complex\u0022 is\nclose). It only relabels findings that are reported anyway, so scores, gate and baseline do not change. Switch it off with --rule\nbrain-method=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022brain-method\u0022: false } in .skarp.json.\n\nDefinition: Skarp\u0027s own, after CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method: all part findings at one member, each at its own threshold.\nParameters (default): parts=long-method,complex-method,deep-nesting, minParts=all; change with --threshold brain-method.\u003Cparameter\u003E=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.brain-method.\u003Cparameter\u003E in .skarp.json.\nOn by default; switch off: --rule brain-method=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022brain-method\u0022: false } in .skarp.json.\nSources: Lanza and Marinescu 2006 (Object-Oriented Metrics in Practice), CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method and Brain Class.",
                "markdown": "**brain-method: Brain method (composite) (judged per member)**\n\nA member that is long, complex and deeply nested at once, reported as one finding listing those parts; on by default.\n\nWhat it measures: a member that has all of the long-method, complex-method and deep-nesting findings at once (each at its own threshold in effect: by default over 50 physical lines, cognitive complexity over 15, nesting depth over 3). It finds nothing the parts do not find: it groups them under one name. The part set is the rule parameter parts (comma-separated member rules: long-method, complex-method, deep-nesting, bumpy-road, complex-condition, too-many-parameters, path-explosion) and minParts (how many of them must fire; default all). Lanza and Marinescu\u0027s Brain Method (2006) adds a fourth leg, many accessed variables; skarp leaves it out at the syntax tier, as CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method (size, complexity, nesting) does.\n\nWhy it matters: a method that is long, branchy and deeply nested at once tends to hold the logic of its class in one place (\u0022centralizes the functionality\u0022, Lanza and Marinescu): it is the hardest kind of method to read, test and change safely, and it attracts more change.\n\nHow to improve: split it. Extract the commented or blank-line separated steps into methods with names that say what they do (Extract Method); flatten the nesting with guard clauses, early returns and continue; move each branch of a large if/switch into its own method or a lookup table; pull loop bodies out into methods. Re-run skarp: each part that drops below its threshold leaves the composite, and it disappears when a part is gone. How it is reported: one line in place of its parts (the text format, Markdown, GitHub annotations and skarp check), listing them; --all on skarp analyze and skarp show \u003Chandle\u003E list the parts one by one. The JSON keeps every part finding in findings and adds the composite to the composites list (rule, place, severity, status, part ids); SARIF reports it with its parts as related locations, next to the parts\u0027 own results. The composite has no threshold, score weight or gate entry of its own: the baseline records the parts, the score charges the parts once, and the gate judges the parts. Its severity is the highest of its parts; against the baseline it is known when every part is known, new when every part is new, and worsened otherwise.\n\nSuppression: [SuppressMessage(\u0022skarp\u0022, \u0022brain-method\u0022, Justification = \u0022\u003Cwhy\u003E\u0022)] on the member or type hides the composite and all its parts there. Suppressing one part hides that part only; the composite stays when the remaining findings still meet its definition.\n\nOn by default (CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method is on in its C# rules, NDepend\u0027s \u0022Avoid methods too big, too complex\u0022 is close). It only relabels findings that are reported anyway, so scores, gate and baseline do not change. Switch it off with --rule brain-method=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022brain-method\u0022: false } in .skarp.json.\n\nDefinition: Skarp\u0027s own, after CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method: all part findings at one member, each at its own threshold. Parameters (default): parts=long-method,complex-method,deep-nesting, minParts=all; change with --threshold brain-method.\u003Cparameter\u003E=\u003Cvalue\u003E on skarp analyze or baseline, or thresholds.brain-method.\u003Cparameter\u003E in .skarp.json. On by default; switch off: --rule brain-method=off on skarp analyze or baseline, or \u0022rules\u0022: { \u0022brain-method\u0022: false } in .skarp.json. Sources: Lanza and Marinescu 2006 (Object-Oriented Metrics in Practice), CodeScene\u0027s Brain Method and Brain Class."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "maintainability",
                  "syntax"
                ],
                "problem.severity": "recommendation",
                "unit": "member"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
